// Five original hard AgilePM v3 Foundation papers (26–30).
// Official APMG style + syllabus weighting. Not official APMG items.
// `correct` is the exact option string (fails closed if options are edited without updating it).

export const FINAL_MOCKS_F = [
  {
    id: 'final26',
    title: 'Final Paper 26',
    questions: [
      { id: 'F26Q1', topic: 'KC', q: 'Frederick Winslow Taylor\'s Scientific Management is criticised in AgilePM because typical staff were expected to:', options: ['Challenge every procedure and redesign the operating model each week', 'Hold the project budget and cancel work that lacked a Business Case', 'Follow strict procedures as operators of systems, without needing to understand the reasoning behind the rules', 'Tailor Scrum\'s roles, events and artefacts before the first Sprint'], correct: 'Follow strict procedures as operators of systems, without needing to understand the reasoning behind the rules', explain: 'Taylorism promoted efficiency, automation and strict procedure-following. Staff acted as operators and were not expected to understand the why; in extremes they were treated like machines.' },
      { id: 'F26Q2', topic: 'KC', q: 'Identify the missing year. James Martin introduced Rapid Application Development (RAD) in [ ? ], using close user-developer working, demonstrations and short feedback loops rather than a formalised specification.', options: ['1991', '1994', '2001', '2010'], correct: '1991', explain: 'RAD is James Martin, 1991. DSDM followed in 1994 to restore control and quality that RAD often sacrificed.' },
      { id: 'F26Q3', topic: 'KC', q: 'Which of the following correctly describe why DSDM was launched in 1994?\n1. Waterfall approaches were too slow and too big, with insufficient ongoing organisational involvement\n2. RAD already supplied complete project governance, so DSDM was only a rebrand\n3. RAD\'s speed and quick fixes often harmed quality because teams skipped analysis and design\n4. DSDM combined Waterfall control and quality with RAD\'s iterative development, business involvement and transparency', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'DSDM (1994) addressed Waterfall slowness and RAD\'s quality/scalability gaps by combining control with iterative, business-centred delivery.' },
      { id: 'F26Q4', topic: 'KC', q: 'Consider the statements about framework adaptability.\n1. Scrum treats its roles, artefacts, events and rules as fixed; the core elements must all be present for a team to be using Scrum effectively\n2. AgilePM guidance must be applied unaltered in every organisation, because tailoring is treated as a failure of discipline', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 1 is true', explain: 'Scrum\'s structure is fixed. AgilePM is intended to be tailored to context; that flexibility is expected and desirable.' },
      { id: 'F26Q5', topic: 'KC', q: 'AgilePM uses the term latent value for the potential value of a product. When is that value actually realised?', options: ['When the Product Backlog is first ordered', 'When the product is actually in use', 'When the Terms of Reference is signed', 'When a single-figure estimate is chosen'], correct: 'When the product is actually in use', explain: 'Latent value is potential value, realised only in operational use. A solution-focused view exists to capture that value, not merely to ship a product.' },
      { id: 'F26Q6', topic: 'SC', q: 'Which commitment is attached to the Sprint Backlog?', options: ['Product Goal', 'Definition of Done', 'Solution Goal', 'Sprint Goal'], correct: 'Sprint Goal', explain: 'Product Backlog commits to the Product Goal; Sprint Backlog to the Sprint Goal; Increment to the Definition of Done.' },
      { id: 'F26Q7', topic: 'SC', q: 'Which of the following are Scrum artefacts?\n1. Product Backlog\n2. Sprint Backlog\n3. Delivery Plan\n4. Increment', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'Scrum artefacts are Product Backlog, Sprint Backlog and Increment. The Delivery Plan is an AgilePM Project Artifact.' },
      { id: 'F26Q8', topic: 'SC', q: 'Identify the missing words. Adaptation must occur [ ? ] to minimise further deviation, and it becomes harder when people are not empowered or self-managing.', options: ['only at the project retrospective', 'as soon as possible', 'after the Business Sponsor signs a change request', 'once the Cone of Uncertainty has closed'], correct: 'as soon as possible', explain: 'Inspection enables adaptation. Adaptation must happen as soon as possible; it is harder without empowerment and self-management.' },
      { id: 'F26Q9', topic: 'SC', q: 'Consider the statements about cancelling a Sprint.\n1. Only the Product Owner may cancel a Sprint, and only if the Sprint Goal has become obsolete\n2. The Scrum Master may cancel a Sprint if a Daily Scrum overruns its timebox', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 1 is true', explain: 'Cancellation is solely the Product Owner\'s decision, and only when the Sprint Goal is obsolete. Timebox overrun is not a cancellation trigger.' },
      { id: 'F26Q10', topic: 'SC', q: 'What three topics does Sprint Planning address, in order?', options: ['Who is at fault; what slipped; how to report it', 'How first; who second; when last', 'Why is this Sprint valuable; what can be Done this Sprint; how will the chosen work get done', 'Cost; quality; features'], correct: 'Why is this Sprint valuable; what can be Done this Sprint; how will the chosen work get done', explain: 'Sprint Planning covers Why (Sprint Goal), What (selected items) and How (plan, often decomposing work to a day or less).' },
      { id: 'F26Q11', topic: 'SC', q: 'Scrum is founded on which pair of ideas?', options: ['Empiricism and lean thinking', 'Taylorism and stage-gate funding', 'Contract negotiation and comprehensive documentation', 'Command-and-control and single-figure estimates'], correct: 'Empiricism and lean thinking', explain: 'Empiricism says knowledge comes from experience and observed results. Lean thinking reduces waste and focuses on essentials.' },
      { id: 'F26Q12', topic: 'AF', q: 'In the AgilePM treatment of the four project variables, which set is treated as FIXED?', options: ['Features, Time and Cost, with Quality left variable', 'Time, Cost and Quality, with Features variable', 'Time only, with Cost, Quality and Features all variable', 'Quality and Features, with Time and Cost variable'], correct: 'Time, Cost and Quality, with Features variable', explain: 'AgilePM fixes Time, Cost and Quality and flexes Features. Quality is protected by a clear Definition of Done rather than becoming an accidental variable.' },
      { id: 'F26Q13', topic: 'AF', q: 'Identify the missing principle. [ ? ] requires every decision to be evaluated against the project vision, focused on delivering what the organisation needs at the right time.', options: ['Demonstrate Control', 'Develop Iteratively', 'Focus on the Business Need', 'Never Compromise Quality'], correct: 'Focus on the Business Need', explain: 'Principle 1: the Business Visionary defines the vision; every decision is tested against that need and timing.' },
      { id: 'F26Q14', topic: 'AF', q: 'Which of the following are AgilePM Principles?\n1. Collaborate\n2. Build Incrementally from Firm Foundations\n3. Communicate Continuously and Clearly\n4. Maximise work in progress across all teams', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'The eight principles include Collaborate, Build Incrementally from Firm Foundations, and Communicate Continuously and Clearly. Maximising WIP is not a principle.' },
      { id: 'F26Q15', topic: 'AF', q: 'Consider the statements about what underpins AgilePM.\n1. Common sense and pragmatism sit beneath the four foundational elements of the framework\n2. Pragmatism means making decisions that adapt to real-life situations rather than following rigid rules', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Process, People, Project Artifacts and Practices sit on common sense and pragmatism: logical judgement and adapting to reality rather than rigid procedure.' },
      { id: 'F26Q16', topic: 'AF', q: 'Which danger does AgilePM associate with artificial deadlines that do not align with real business needs?', options: ['They automatically raise the Must Have share above 60%', 'They convert Could Haves into Must Haves', 'They replace the need for a Business Case', 'They can be harmful if used only as a measure of success or control'], correct: 'They can be harmful if used only as a measure of success or control', explain: 'Deliver on Time is about real business timing. Artificial deadlines used only for control can damage the project.' },
      { id: 'F26Q17', topic: 'IN', q: 'Equal authority in AgilePM is shared by which trio of Project Roles?', options: ['Business Sponsor, Business Visionary and Solution Architect', 'Project Manager, Scrum Master and Product Owner', 'Business Sponsor, Project Manager and Developers', 'Business Visionary, Scrum Master and Process Advisor'], correct: 'Business Sponsor, Business Visionary and Solution Architect', explain: 'Equal authority sits with Business Sponsor, Business Visionary and Solution Architect. The Project Manager does not share that tier.' },
      { id: 'F26Q18', topic: 'IN', q: 'A Product Owner in AgilePM maintains four named stakeholder relationships. Which names belong on that list?\n1. Business Sponsor\n2. Business Visionary\n3. Solution Architect\n4. Project Manager', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '2, 3, 4', explain: 'The four relationships are Business Visionary, Solution Architect, Project Manager, and Business and Technical Advisors. The Business Sponsor is not one of the four.' },
      { id: 'F26Q19', topic: 'IN', q: 'Identify the missing role. Budget-holding, Business Case ownership and final authority on critical escalations sit with the [ ? ], the project\'s champion.', options: ['Business Sponsor', 'Product Owner', 'Scrum Master', 'Process Advisor'], correct: 'Business Sponsor', explain: 'The Business Sponsor is the Project Champion: owns the Business Case, holds the budget, and is the final authority on critical escalated decisions.' },
      { id: 'F26Q20', topic: 'IN', q: 'Consider the statements about sharing the Business Sponsor role.\n1. On a simple project the Business Sponsor role is routinely split equally across several people\n2. The role is fulfilled by a single person, though in complex contexts that person may represent a governing group', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 2 is true', explain: 'The Business Sponsor is always one person. In complex settings they may represent a steering or investment group, but the role itself is not shared.' },
      { id: 'F26Q21', topic: 'IN', q: 'The Solution Architect balances which other role\'s ambitions so that the solution remains realistic and feasible?', options: ['Scrum Master', 'Process Advisor', 'Project Manager', 'Business Visionary'], correct: 'Business Visionary', explain: 'The Solution Architect owns architecture and the delivery approach and balances the Business Visionary\'s ambitions so the solution stays feasible.' },
      { id: 'F26Q22', topic: 'IN', q: 'Which of the following are Project Manager accountabilities?\n1. Establishing and managing an effective project environment in accordance with AgilePM\n2. Creating the Business Case in place of the Value Ownership roles\n3. Inspecting progress against the Delivery Plan and adapting as necessary\n4. Managing escalated issues and acting as final authority on delivery-enablement decisions', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'The Project Manager does not create the Business Case. Value Ownership owns it; the Project Manager ensures it receives attention.' },
      { id: 'F26Q23', topic: 'IN', q: 'Geoff Watts\' two commandments for a Scrum Master are:', options: ['Estimate the backlog and approve the budget', 'First ask the team and make yourself redundant', 'Cancel weak Sprints and rewrite the Definition of Done nightly', 'Direct the Developers and report only to finance'], correct: 'First ask the team and make yourself redundant', explain: 'Watts: default to asking the team, and coach toward a self-managing team so the Scrum Master becomes unnecessary.' },
      { id: 'F26Q24', topic: 'CC', q: 'Which Agile Manifesto principle identifies the most efficient and effective method of conveying information to and within a team?', options: ['Working solutions are the primary measure of progress', 'Simplicity — maximising the amount of work not done', 'Welcome changing requirements, even late', 'Face-to-face conversation'], correct: 'Face-to-face conversation', explain: 'Manifesto principle 6: face-to-face is the most efficient and effective method within a team.' },
      { id: 'F26Q25', topic: 'CC', q: 'Which of the following are listed benefits of collaboration in AgilePM?\n1. Improved problem-solving\n2. Improved learning\n3. Increased productivity\n4. Reduced need for a shared purpose', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'Benefits include problem-solving, learning, productivity and cross-functional learning. A shared purpose is a prerequisite, not something collaboration removes.' },
      { id: 'F26Q26', topic: 'CC', q: 'Identify the missing words. On the Power/Interest Grid, a stakeholder with high power and low interest should be [ ? ].', options: ['closely engaged as a Delivery Team member', 'kept satisfied', 'monitored with minimum effort', 'ignored until Deployment'], correct: 'kept satisfied', explain: 'High power / low interest = keep satisfied. High/high = closely engage. Low/low = monitor. Low power / high interest = keep informed.' },
      { id: 'F26Q27', topic: 'CC', q: 'Consider the statements about communication choices.\n1. Email is recommended as the primary method for complex design decisions\n2. Face-to-face conversation is the least effective method in small or medium groups', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true', explain: 'Face-to-face is the most effective method in small or medium groups. Email is best used to confirm earlier discussions, not as the primary design channel.' },
      { id: 'F26Q28', topic: 'PR', q: 'Which increment type is a stepwise refinement of a product, delivered by one or more Agile Delivery Teams, that meets the agreed Definition of Done?', options: ['Product Increment', 'Solution Increment', 'Project Increment', 'Deployed Solution'], correct: 'Product Increment', explain: 'A Product Increment is what Delivery Teams build. A Solution Increment combines products with other items (training, change, communications) needed for the project outcome.' },
      { id: 'F26Q29', topic: 'PR', q: 'Which of the following are phases of the AgilePM Process Model?\n1. Pre-Project\n2. Feasibility\n3. Contract Negotiation\n4. Realisation', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'The six phases are Pre-Project, Feasibility, Foundations, Delivery, Deployment and Realisation. Contract negotiation is not a lifecycle phase.' },
      { id: 'F26Q30', topic: 'PR', q: 'Identify the missing artifact. The [ ? ] is a short milestone document used in Pre-Project to present a new project idea and support project governance requirements.', options: ['Sprint Backlog', 'Benefits Assessment', 'Product Increment', 'Terms of Reference'], correct: 'Terms of Reference', explain: 'The Terms of Reference is a Recommended milestone artifact. Its main purpose is to justify a Feasibility Phase, not to prove feasibility itself.' },
      { id: 'F26Q31', topic: 'PR', q: 'Consider the statements about the Feasibility Assessment.\n1. It can act as a management summary of the Business Case, SAD, DAD and MAD in one report\n2. It can alternatively replace those artifacts by combining their key details into one report', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'The Feasibility Assessment is an Optional milestone snapshot used for governance go/no-go. It may summarise or replace the key Feasibility artifacts.' },
      { id: 'F26Q32', topic: 'PR', q: 'The DAD artifact is the project\'s agreed how-we-build record. What content does it mainly carry?', options: ['The investment appraisal that justifies the project', 'How the project and Delivery Team(s) will build the solution, including ways of working for development, quality assurance and delivery', 'The ordered list of Product Backlog items for the next Sprint', 'Observed benefits after the solution has been in live use'], correct: 'How the project and Delivery Team(s) will build the solution, including ways of working for development, quality assurance and delivery', explain: 'DAD is the evolutionary how-we-build artifact, including the quality-assurance strategy. SAD is the design framework; MAD is how the project is managed.' },
      { id: 'F26Q33', topic: 'ES', q: 'AgilePM advises keeping Must Have effort at or below which share of the total, so that Should and Could Haves can provide contingency?', options: ['20%', '40%', '60%', '80%'], correct: '60%', explain: 'Must Haves should be no more than about 60% of effort. Could Haves are typically around 20% and form the main contingency buffer.' },
      { id: 'F26Q34', topic: 'ES', q: 'Which of the following correctly describe a Could Have?\n1. It is guaranteed to be delivered if it appears on the Solution Backlog\n2. It is wanted or desirable but less important than a Should Have\n3. It provides the main pool of contingency if the deadline is at risk\n4. AgilePM typically allocates about 20% of total effort to Could Haves', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '2, 3, 4', explain: 'Could Haves are the first drop and are not guaranteed. Only Must Haves are guaranteed as the Minimum Useable Subset.' },
      { id: 'F26Q35', topic: 'ES', q: 'Identify the missing case. In MoSCoW-based financial framing, the [ ? ] benefits case is based on Must Haves plus Should Haves plus Could Haves.', options: ['best', 'worst', 'expected', 'sunk'], correct: 'best', explain: 'Worst case = Musts only. Expected = Musts + Shoulds. Best = Musts + Shoulds + Coulds. Costs still cover the full in-scope set.' },
      { id: 'F26Q36', topic: 'ES', q: 'Consider the statements about MoSCoW.\n1. A Must Have cannot depend on a Should Have or Could Have, because those items might not be delivered\n2. MoSCoW mandates the sequence in which requirements must be built, regardless of dependencies', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 1 is true', explain: 'MoSCoW describes importance, not order of delivery. A Must Have may depend only on other Must Haves.' },
      { id: 'F26Q37', topic: 'ES', q: 'Which of the following are INVEST criteria?\n1. Independent\n2. Negotiable\n3. Integrated\n4. Testable', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'INVEST = Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable. Integrated is not a letter.' },
      { id: 'F26Q38', topic: 'ES', q: 'Who is responsible for estimating the size of Product Backlog items?', options: ['The Business Sponsor alone', 'The Project Manager, so that finance can lock a single figure', 'The Process Advisor', 'The Developers; the Product Owner may guide with business context and trade-offs'], correct: 'The Developers; the Product Owner may guide with business context and trade-offs', explain: 'The people doing the work estimate it. The Product Owner clarifies value and trade-offs but does not impose the size.' },
      { id: 'F26Q39', topic: 'PC', q: 'Which of the following is one of AgilePM\'s three planning concepts?', options: ['Fixing every task in a Gantt chart before Feasibility ends', 'Planning to sensible horizons', 'Using burn-down charts to rank Delivery Teams', 'Escalating every defect to the Business Sponsor'], correct: 'Planning to sensible horizons', explain: 'The three planning concepts are outcome-based planning, planning to sensible horizons, and plan and re-plan using the most up-to-date estimates.' },
      { id: 'F26Q40', topic: 'PC', q: 'Which of the following are AgilePM tracking and control concepts?\n1. Outcome-based measurement\n2. Comprehensive documentation before any increment\n3. Transparency of process and progress\n4. Management by exception', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'The four control concepts are outcome-based measurement, transparency of process and progress, responding to change, and management by exception.' },
      { id: 'F26Q41', topic: 'PC', q: 'Identify the missing words. An exception arises if the Delivery Team determines that the [ ? ] is at risk — for example if agreed Must Haves will not be delivered.', options: ['coffee-cup estimate', 'email protocol', 'Sprint Goal', 'Terms of Reference wording'], correct: 'Sprint Goal', explain: 'Not achieving the Sprint Goal (including agreed Must Haves) is a major deviation and must be escalated. Day-to-day variance stays with the team.' },
      { id: 'F26Q42', topic: 'PC', q: 'Consider the statements about progress charts.\n1. Burn-down and burn-up charts should be used to judge and compare team performance\n2. Those charts are working tools for visibility, inspection and adaptation for an individual team', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 2 is true', explain: 'Charts support empiricism for that team. They must not be used to rank or judge teams against one another.' },
      { id: 'F26Q43', topic: 'PC', q: 'In a multi-team kick-off that mirrors Topics 1 and 2 of Sprint Planning, which role usually facilitates with the Product Owner and team representatives present?', options: ['The Agile Project Manager', 'The Business Sponsor', 'An external auditor', 'The Process Advisor acting as Product Owner'], correct: 'The Agile Project Manager', explain: 'Project Sprint Planning is facilitated by the Project Manager with PO and team representatives. It mirrors the first two topics of standard Sprint Planning.' },
      { id: 'F26Q44', topic: 'RK', q: 'Which roles correctly own the stated risk category?\n1. Business Visionary owns Value Ownership-based risk\n2. Solution Architect owns Solution Delivery-based risk\n3. Project Manager owns Delivery Enablement risk, including organisation, schedule and dependencies\n4. The Scrum Master alone owns all three risk categories', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'Risk ownership follows the three role domains. The Scrum Master does not absorb all project risk.' },
      { id: 'F26Q45', topic: 'RK', q: 'Identify the missing word. If any PAQ statement is considered not to be true, it indicates a [ ? ] to using AgilePM successfully, and the approach should be tailored and the risk mitigated.', options: ['guarantee of project success', 'reason to abandon MoSCoW', 'requirement to fix scope', 'risk'], correct: 'risk', explain: 'Disagreement with a PAQ statement is approach-based risk. Tailor and mitigate; do not blindly run the default approach.' },
      { id: 'F26Q46', topic: 'RK', q: 'Consider the statements about PAQ timing.\n1. The PAQ is typically first used in Feasibility to surface approach-based risk early\n2. The PAQ should be reviewed again at the end of Foundations, and whenever Foundations is revisited', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'First pass in Feasibility; review at the end of Foundations and on later Foundations passes so earlier tailoring remains valid.' },
      { id: 'F26Q47', topic: 'GV', q: 'In AgilePM, governance in the project context is best defined as:', options: ['Adapting rules only when an auditor is absent', 'Replacing empiricism with stage-gate documents', 'The set of processes, structures and controls that ensure a project is well managed and meeting its objective', 'A ban on inspecting increments until the final Deployment'], correct: 'The set of processes, structures and controls that ensure a project is well managed and meeting its objective', explain: 'Governance is those processes, structures and controls. Empiricism is learning from experience and observed results. They need not conflict if governance serves its purpose.' },
      { id: 'F26Q48', topic: 'GV', q: 'Appendix A RACI for early approach artifacts: which pairings are correct?\n1. Terms of Reference — Responsible: Business Sponsor; Accountable: Business Visionary\n2. Solution Architecture Definition — Responsible: Solution Architect; Accountable: Business Visionary\n3. Delivery Approach Definition — Responsible: Solution Architect; Accountable: Project Manager\n4. Management Approach Definition — Responsible: Project Manager; Accountable: Business Sponsor', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '2, 3, 4', explain: 'ToR is Responsible Business Visionary and Accountable Business Sponsor — statement 1 swaps them. SAD R=SA A=BV; DAD R=SA A=PM; MAD R=PM A=BS.' },
      { id: 'F26Q49', topic: 'GV', q: 'Identify the missing pairing. Governance is often mistaken as the opposite of [ ? ], yet the two coexist when controls serve the project\'s objective without blocking learning from what is observed.', options: ['empiricism', 'MoSCoW', 'timeboxing', 'facilitation'], correct: 'empiricism', explain: 'They look like opposites only if governance means rigid rule-following. Purpose-led governance can support empirical inspect-and-adapt.' },
      { id: 'F26Q50', topic: 'GV', q: 'Consider the statements about regulatory evidence.\n1. An auditor will always accept a Sprint Backlog as a valid plan without any explanation\n2. Many regulations require traceability but do not prescribe that it must be a traditional document', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 2 is true', explain: 'Understand the intent of the standard, then meet it in an agile way. Auditors may not recognise agile plans unless the team explains what valid evidence looks like.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'final27',
    title: 'Final Paper 27',
    questions: [
      { id: 'F27Q1', topic: 'KC', q: 'Identify the missing name. [ ? ] is the Manifesto signatory specifically identified as part of the team behind AgilePM.', options: ['Frederick Winslow Taylor', 'James Martin', 'Barry Boehm', 'Arie van Bennekum'], correct: 'Arie van Bennekum', explain: 'Arie van Bennekum signed the Agile Manifesto and is identified with the DSDM/AgilePM lineage. Taylor, Martin and Boehm are other historical figures in the guide.' },
      { id: 'F27Q2', topic: 'KC', q: 'Which of the following correctly contrast RAD (1991) with DSDM (1994)?\n1. RAD relied on discussions, demonstrations and short feedback loops rather than a formalised specification\n2. RAD introduced new problems of support, scale and quality because teams often moved straight into development\n3. DSDM discarded business involvement in favour of specialist-only design\n4. DSDM brought together Waterfall control and quality with RAD\'s iterative, transparent, business-involved working', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'DSDM kept business involvement and added the control/quality missing from RAD. It did not revert to specialist-only design.' },
      { id: 'F27Q3', topic: 'KC', q: 'Consider the statements about Taylorism.\n1. Scientific Management promoted efficiency, automation, economies of scale and strict procedure-following\n2. AgilePM presents Taylorism as the preferred operating model for VUCA work because operators need not think', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 1 is true', explain: 'Taylorism is the 20th-century contrast. VUCA needs people who can think, assess and adapt — the opposite of treating staff as machines.' },
      { id: 'F27Q4', topic: 'KC', q: 'Why does AgilePM distinguish product development from project management?', options: ['Product development organises the wider environment; project management only writes user stories', 'They are identical once Scrum is adopted', 'Project management forbids incremental delivery', 'Product development focuses on what the product is and how it is built and improved; project management organises the environment so that delivered value is actually achieved'], correct: 'Product development focuses on what the product is and how it is built and improved; project management organises the environment so that delivered value is actually achieved', explain: 'Product work is the what/how of the product. Project management covers who, why, when and where so value is realised after delivery.' },
      { id: 'F27Q5', topic: 'KC', q: 'A solution-focused view is urged in AgilePM primarily so that:', options: ['The Product Owner can be replaced by a committee', 'Must Haves can be estimated as a single hour figure', 'The Sprint Backlog can be owned by the Business Sponsor', 'Latent value is captured when the product is in use, not only when a product increment exists'], correct: 'Latent value is captured when the product is in use, not only when a product increment exists', explain: 'Shipping a product is not the same as realising value. Latent value appears only in use, which is why Solution Increments include the wider change, not just the product.' },
      { id: 'F27Q6', topic: 'SC', q: 'Which of the following are Scrum Values?\n1. Commitment\n2. Focus\n3. Openness\n4. Consensus', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'The five values are Commitment, Focus, Openness, Respect and Courage. Consensus is not a Scrum Value.' },
      { id: 'F27Q7', topic: 'SC', q: 'Identify the missing timebox. For a one-month Sprint, Sprint Planning is timeboxed to a maximum of [ ? ].', options: ['15 minutes', '4 hours', '3 hours', '8 hours'], correct: '8 hours', explain: 'Planning max 8 hours; Review max 4 hours; Retrospective max 3 hours; Daily Scrum 15 minutes. Shorter Sprints use a shorter Planning box.' },
      { id: 'F27Q8', topic: 'SC', q: 'At what instant does AgilePM say a Product Increment comes into existence?', options: ['When the Business Sponsor signs the Terms of Reference', 'When it is listed on the Delivery Plan', 'When a burn-down chart reaches zero, even if tests failed', 'The moment a Product Backlog item meets the Definition of Done'], correct: 'The moment a Product Backlog item meets the Definition of Done', explain: 'An Increment is born when the PBI meets the DoD. If it does not, it cannot be released or presented at the Sprint Review and returns to the Product Backlog.' },
      { id: 'F27Q9', topic: 'SC', q: 'Consider the statements about the Daily Scrum.\n1. It is a 15-minute event for the Developers, held at the same time and place each working day\n2. It is the only time Developers are allowed to adjust their plan during the Sprint', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 1 is true', explain: 'The Daily Scrum inspects progress toward the Sprint Goal. Developers may adapt the plan at other times as well.' },
      { id: 'F27Q10', topic: 'SC', q: 'Which three elements together make up a Sprint Backlog?', options: ['Only the Product Goal and a Gantt chart', 'Only unfinished Feasibility Assessment actions', 'The Management Approach Definition and the project budget', 'The Sprint Goal, the selected Product Backlog items, and an actionable plan for delivering the Product Increment'], correct: 'The Sprint Goal, the selected Product Backlog items, and an actionable plan for delivering the Product Increment', explain: 'Sprint Backlog = why (Sprint Goal) + what (selected PBIs) + how (the plan). Developers create and update it.' },
      { id: 'F27Q11', topic: 'SC', q: 'Transparency without inspection is described in Scrum theory as:', options: ['A complete substitute for adaptation', 'The Definition of Ready', 'A reason to skip the Sprint Review', 'Misleading and wasteful'], correct: 'Misleading and wasteful', explain: 'Transparency enables inspection; inspection without adaptation is pointless; transparency without inspection is misleading and wasteful.' },
      { id: 'F27Q12', topic: 'AF', q: 'Which of the following are foundational elements of the AgilePM Framework?\n1. Process\n2. People\n3. Project Artifacts\n4. Procurement litigation', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'The four elements are Process, People, Project Artifacts and Practices. Common sense and pragmatism underpin them.' },
      { id: 'F27Q13', topic: 'AF', q: 'How does AgilePM typically manage contingency, rather than adding slack to time or budget?', options: ['By extending every Sprint until all Could Haves are Done', 'By making Quality the intentional variable', 'By forbidding any change after Foundations', 'By using MoSCoW so that lower-priority features can be dropped'], correct: 'By using MoSCoW so that lower-priority features can be dropped', explain: 'Time, cost and quality stay fixed. Contingency lives in Should/Could scope, not in Parkinson-prone time or budget pads.' },
      { id: 'F27Q14', topic: 'AF', q: 'Identify the missing words. Documentation should be created only if it adds value, helps solution development and support, or is essential for [ ? ].', options: ['padding the Project Review Report', 'replacing face-to-face conversation', 'keeping Taylorist procedures unchanged', 'safety-critical or regulatory compliance purposes'], correct: 'safety-critical or regulatory compliance purposes', explain: 'Principle 7: create documents only when they add value, aid development/support, or are required for safety or regulation.' },
      { id: 'F27Q15', topic: 'AF', q: 'Consider the statements about AgilePM combined with Scrum.\n1. AgilePM can provide a project lifecycle with financial and regulatory oversight that pure team-level Scrum does not supply\n2. AgilePM can handle business-related work not directly linked to product development, such as business process change', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'AgilePM adds lifecycle, extra roles, multi-team structures and techniques for time/cost, and can manage non-product business change.' },
      { id: 'F27Q16', topic: 'AF', q: 'Under Demonstrate Control, a partially completed feature is said to have:', options: ['The same business value as a finished feature', 'More business value than a feature not started', 'Value equal to its percentage complete on a Gantt chart', 'Less business value than a feature not started, because incomplete work adds complication without value'], correct: 'Less business value than a feature not started, because incomplete work adds complication without value', explain: 'Progress is measured by working solutions. Incomplete work complicates the solution without delivering value, so it is worse than not starting.' },
      { id: 'F27Q17', topic: 'IN', q: 'Identify the missing principle. The first Principle of Agile Leadership is that [ ? ].', options: ['only the Project Manager may decide', 'emotion must be excluded from delivery', 'followers are preferable to new leaders', 'leadership lives everywhere in the organisation'], correct: 'leadership lives everywhere in the organisation', explain: 'Leadership can emerge from expertise and initiative. Agile leaders develop new leaders, not followers.' },
      { id: 'F27Q18', topic: 'IN', q: 'Consider the statements about the Product Owner.\n1. The Product Owner should normally be a committee so that no individual can be blamed\n2. For the Product Owner to succeed, the entire organisation must respect their decisions', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 2 is true', explain: 'The Product Owner is one person representing many stakeholders. A committee is specifically warned against.' },
      { id: 'F27Q19', topic: 'IN', q: 'Which domain does the Scrum Master fit into in AgilePM?', options: ['Value Ownership', 'Solution Delivery', 'External assurance only', 'Delivery Enablement'], correct: 'Delivery Enablement', explain: 'Project Manager is the Project Role in Delivery Enablement. The Scrum Master (Team Leader) sits in that domain and corresponds to the earlier Team Leader role.' },
      { id: 'F27Q20', topic: 'IN', q: 'Which of the following are Advisor Roles in AgilePM?\n1. Business Advisor\n2. Solution Advisor\n3. Process Advisor\n4. Budget Advisor', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'The three Advisor roles are Business, Solution and Process. There is no Budget Advisor title. An Agile Coach is a typical Process Advisor.' },
      { id: 'F27Q21', topic: 'IN', q: 'What do Business Advisors commonly contribute regarding regulation?', options: ['They replace the Business Sponsor as budget holder', 'They own the Sprint Backlog', 'They provide specialist business input on requirements, decisions and regulatory compliance, and may later become a Product Owner or Business Ambassador', 'They cancel Sprints when auditors visit'], correct: 'They provide specialist business input on requirements, decisions and regulatory compliance, and may later become a Product Owner or Business Ambassador', explain: 'Business Advisors support Value Ownership, including compliance. The same person may later take a Product Owner or Ambassador role.' },
      { id: 'F27Q22', topic: 'IN', q: 'Which of the following correctly describe external stakeholders?\n1. They are not directly part of the project organisation but may still influence it or be affected by the solution\n2. They can include employees affected by a change as well as customers, suppliers or regulators\n3. They always join the Delivery Team as Developers\n4. Purpose, timing, authority and constraints influence how they interact', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'External stakeholders sit outside the project organisation. BV and SA manage those relationships, facilitated by the Project Manager.' },
      { id: 'F27Q23', topic: 'IN', q: 'In a scaled setting, good practice suggests an experienced Scrum Master may effectively support up to how many teams?', options: ['One only, in every case', 'Ten, if they share no backlog', 'Three', 'Unlimited, if velocity is high'], correct: 'Three', explain: 'A Scrum Master may support up to three teams. An experienced PO may also support more than one team if they share a single Product Backlog.' },
      { id: 'F27Q24', topic: 'CC', q: 'Projects often fail because of communication breakdowns. Which listed failures are genuine?\n1. Not using a shared language\n2. Lack of access to the right people at the right time\n3. Over-reliance on written communication instead of richer methods\n4. Agreeing methods, style and frequency in advance', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'Not agreeing methods/style/frequency causes confusion. Agreeing them is a remedy, not a failure mode.' },
      { id: 'F27Q25', topic: 'CC', q: 'Which Aligned Autonomy quadrant pairs high alignment with high autonomy?', options: ['Authoritative organisation / authoritarian culture', 'Bureaucratic organisation / indifferent culture', 'Innovative organisation / collaborative culture', 'Entrepreneurial organisation / chaotic culture'], correct: 'Innovative organisation / collaborative culture', explain: 'High/high is the innovative, collaborative culture AgilePM wants. High alignment / low autonomy is authoritative; low/low is bureaucratic; low alignment / high autonomy is chaotic.' },
      { id: 'F27Q26', topic: 'CC', q: 'Identify the missing words. Active listening includes paying attention, showing you are listening, observing feelings, giving constructive feedback, and [ ? ] until the speaker has finished.', options: ['interrupting with a solution', 'forwarding the conversation to email', 'deferring judgement', 'escalating to the Business Sponsor'], correct: 'deferring judgement', explain: 'Active listening: attend, show listening, observe feelings, give constructive feedback, defer judgement, then respond appropriately.' },
      { id: 'F27Q27', topic: 'CC', q: 'Consider the statements about documents.\n1. The main goal of a document is a shared understanding of its content, not the existence of the file itself\n2. A verbal commitment made in a group can be stronger than merely signing a document', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Understanding and commitment matter more than paperwork. Documents are created only when they add value or prove compliance.' },
      { id: 'F27Q28', topic: 'PR', q: 'Identify the missing increment. A [ ? ] is a stepwise refinement of the overall solution that meets the agreed Definition of Done and may combine several products with training, communications and business change.', options: ['Product Increment', 'Daily Scrum', 'Solution Increment', 'Planning Poker deck'], correct: 'Solution Increment', explain: 'Product Increments come from Delivery Teams. Solution Increments assemble products plus the other deliverables needed for the project outcome.' },
      { id: 'F27Q29', topic: 'PR', q: 'Which of the following correctly describe Project Increment timeboxes?\n1. They are bound at the start by revisiting Foundations\n2. They are bound at the end by a Project Increment Review\n3. They replace Sprints, so Delivery Timeboxes are no longer used\n4. Each may include Delivery, Deployment and Realisation in various combinations', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'Project Increments nest Delivery Timeboxes (Sprints). They do not replace Sprints.' },
      { id: 'F27Q30', topic: 'PR', q: 'During Feasibility, cost estimates are typically only rough. Which accuracy illustration does AgilePM give at that stage?', options: ['Exact to the nearest hour', 'Zero uncertainty after the PAQ is signed', 'Plus or minus about 50%', 'Best-case benefits only'], correct: 'Plus or minus about 50%', explain: 'Feasibility stays light: just enough to go to Foundations or stop. Further detail is deferred; only rough cost ranges are needed.' },
      { id: 'F27Q31', topic: 'PR', q: 'Consider the statements about the Management Approach Definition.\n1. MAD describes how the project will be organised and managed, including quality, assurance, governance and risk\n2. In a regulated environment MAD can be referenced as a formal description of the process', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'MAD is Recommended and evolutionary, Responsible Project Manager, Accountable Business Sponsor, and is baselined at the end of Foundations.' },
      { id: 'F27Q32', topic: 'PR', q: 'Pre-Project should stop once it has achieved which outcome?', options: ['To prove the full solution architecture in detail', 'To baseline every Product Backlog item', 'To get approval for a small initial investment to explore whether the project is worth pursuing, not to evaluate full feasibility', 'To measure Benefits Assessment after go-live'], correct: 'To get approval for a small initial investment to explore whether the project is worth pursuing, not to evaluate full feasibility', explain: 'Pre-Project decides whether it is worth exploring further and when to start. Feasibility, not Pre-Project, tests desirability, feasibility and viability.' },
      { id: 'F27Q33', topic: 'ES', q: 'Identify the missing words. A change in the [ ? ] of a requirement does not change project scope and should be handled using MoSCoW contingency planned in Foundations.', options: ['sponsoring department', 'RACI colour', 'depth', 'Sprint number only'], correct: 'depth', explain: 'Depth changes are extra detail inside agreed scope and consume MoSCoW contingency. Breadth changes (scope creep) must be formally controlled.' },
      { id: 'F27Q34', topic: 'ES', q: 'Which User Story format is the widely used template referenced by AgilePM?', options: ['Given / When / Then as the whole story', 'Must / Should / Could as the story text', 'As a ... I want ... so that ...', 'RACI / RAID / RASCI'], correct: 'As a ... I want ... so that ...', explain: 'The common format is As a / I want / so that. Given / When / Then is a typical Acceptance Criteria style, not the story template itself.' },
      { id: 'F27Q35', topic: 'ES', q: 'Which of the following are true of Feasibility-phase estimates?\n1. They are rough and based on limited information\n2. Delivery Teams can already give solid, commitment-grade estimates for every Sprint\n3. Analogy techniques such as T-shirt sizing are commonly used\n4. Cost and benefit figures are given as ranges rather than exact numbers', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'Solid estimates come only after Foundations. Feasibility uses rough analogy and ranges to outline the Business Case.' },
      { id: 'F27Q36', topic: 'ES', q: 'Consider the statements about Won\'t Have this time.\n1. Won\'t Haves are documented so they are not later mistaken for forgotten or new work\n2. Won\'t Haves are included in the total effort calculation used to balance Must/Should/Could', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 1 is true', explain: 'Documenting Won\'t Haves manages expectations. They are out of this timeframe and excluded from the effort balance.' },
      { id: 'F27Q37', topic: 'ES', q: 'When first assigning MoSCoW priorities, which starting tactic helps force justification?', options: ['Start with all requirements as Won\'t Haves and promote them only with strong justification', 'Start with everything as a Must Have and negotiate downwards only after Deployment', 'Let the Project Manager assign every priority in isolation', 'Hide Won\'t Haves so stakeholders cannot see them'], correct: 'Start with all requirements as Won\'t Haves and promote them only with strong justification', explain: 'Starting at Won\'t Have forces justification. The Project Manager does not assign priorities; they ensure the plan remains realistic.' },
      { id: 'F27Q38', topic: 'ES', q: 'Which of the following correctly describe estimating styles used with AgilePM and Scrum?\n1. The Project Manager must impose the estimate so finance can lock a single figure\n2. Estimating by analogy compares work to similar past items\n3. Estimating by decomposition breaks work into smaller parts and estimates each\n4. Using both styles together allows estimates to be cross-checked', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '2, 3, 4', explain: 'Analogy (comparative) and decomposition (bottom-up) are the two common styles. The people doing the work estimate it; the PM must not impose sizes.' },
      { id: 'F27Q39', topic: 'PC', q: 'Which of the following are AgilePM planning time levels?\n1. Long-term planning across the project and post-project activities\n2. Minute-by-minute planning of every Developer action in Pre-Project\n3. Medium-term planning for the first or next Project Increment\n4. Short-term planning via Sprint Planning', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'Horizons are long (project), medium (increment) and short (Sprint). Pre-Project does not plan Developer minutes.' },
      { id: 'F27Q40', topic: 'PC', q: 'For a shared cadence across Delivery Teams, which Sprint length is most commonly recommended?', options: ['Two weeks', 'One day', 'Six months', 'Until the Product Goal is abandoned'], correct: 'Two weeks', explain: 'Two weeks is the most common cadence. One to four weeks is normal; six weeks is the maximum recommended length.' },
      { id: 'F27Q41', topic: 'PC', q: 'Identify the missing event. [ ? ] is a joint review at the end of a Sprint when multiple teams have been producing Product Increments; it is a working session, not a grilling by management.', options: ['Project Sprint Review', 'Daily Scrum', 'Planning Poker', 'Pre-Project conversation'], correct: 'Project Sprint Review', explain: 'The Project Sprint Review inspects the Sprint and collaborates on what to do next. The PM may facilitate with SM and PO as co-facilitators. Max 4 hours for a one-month Sprint.' },
      { id: 'F27Q42', topic: 'PC', q: 'Consider the statements about the Project Sprint Retrospective.\n1. It is a joint retrospective for the whole Project Organisation including the Business Sponsor and Business Visionary as mandatory decision-makers\n2. The Business Sponsor and Business Visionary are excluded from the Project Sprint Retrospective', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 2 is true', explain: 'After team retros, the project retro includes the Project Organisation except the Business Sponsor and Business Visionary.' },
      { id: 'F27Q43', topic: 'PC', q: 'Cross-Team Refinement is typically focused on keeping Product Backlog items ready and may also inspect Project Artifacts. What duration does AgilePM suggest teams experiment with?', options: ['15 minutes only', '1 to 3 hours', 'A full calendar month', 'The entire Foundations Phase'], correct: '1 to 3 hours', explain: 'CTR at least once per Sprint, typically 1–3 hours, with Project Roles, POs and team representatives. It can reduce the need to return to Foundations.' },
      { id: 'F27Q44', topic: 'RK', q: 'When applied correctly, AgilePM usually integrates risk into daily work. A separate formal risk process is most likely needed when:', options: ['A Facilitated Workshop is used', 'The environment is highly regulated or safety-related', 'Could Haves exist on the backlog', 'The Daily Scrum lasts 15 minutes'], correct: 'The environment is highly regulated or safety-related', explain: 'VUCA risk is handled by the approach itself. Highly regulated or safety contexts may need intentional risk processes, designed into MAD, SAD and DAD and into DoD/Acceptance Criteria.' },
      { id: 'F27Q45', topic: 'RK', q: 'Which of the following are constraint types called out under PAQ Statement 17?\n1. Fibonacci card design\n2. Internal standards\n3. External (regulatory) standards\n4. Commercial arrangements', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '2, 3, 4', explain: 'Statement 17 concerns mandatory standards or other constraints: internal, external/regulatory, and commercial (for example fixed-price, fixed-scope contracts).' },
      { id: 'F27Q46', topic: 'RK', q: 'Consider the statements about PAQ Statement 2.\n1. The project needs strong, proactive Business Sponsor and Business Visionary ownership\n2. If reorganisation and coaching fail to engage the Business Visionary, the suggested action is to refuse to continue until ownership is resolved', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Weak Value Ownership starves the project of resources and forces risky assumptions. Continuing without an engaged Visionary is not recommended.' },
      { id: 'F27Q47', topic: 'GV', q: 'Identify the missing roles. Appendix A gives the Product Backlog to [ ? ] as Responsible and [ ? ] as Accountable.', options: ['Developers; Product Owner', 'Product Owner; Business Visionary', 'Project Manager; Business Sponsor', 'Business Sponsor; Scrum Master'], correct: 'Product Owner; Business Visionary', explain: 'Product Backlog: R=Product Owner, A=Business Visionary. Sprint Backlog: R=Developers, A=Product Owner. Do not swap these pairs.' },
      { id: 'F27Q48', topic: 'GV', q: 'Using RACI, which increment and backlog ownership pairs are stated correctly?\n1. Product Backlog — Responsible: Business Sponsor; Accountable: Developers\n2. Sprint Backlog — Responsible: Developers; Accountable: Product Owner\n3. Product Increment — Responsible: Delivery Team; Accountable: Product Owner\n4. Solution Increment — Responsible: Project Organisation; Accountable: Business Visionary', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '2, 3, 4', explain: 'Product Backlog is R=PO A=BV, not Sponsor/Developers. Increments follow Delivery Team/PO versus Project Organisation/BV.' },
      { id: 'F27Q49', topic: 'GV', q: 'Compliance-based governance that reverses the Manifesto is characterised by valuing which of the following more highly?', options: ['Individuals and interactions, working solutions, customer collaboration and responding to change', 'Processes and tools, comprehensive documentation, contract negotiation and following a plan', 'Empiricism, lean thinking and self-management', 'Latent value and last-responsible-moment detail'], correct: 'Processes and tools, comprehensive documentation, contract negotiation and following a plan', explain: 'That list is the Manifesto in reverse. Applied as governance, it undermines agility even if the team is trying to work empirically.' },
      { id: 'F27Q50', topic: 'GV', q: 'Consider the statements about financial estimates.\n1. If a Business Sponsor insists on a single-figure estimate, the estimator must pick the figure so that blame sits with the Delivery Team\n2. The Business Sponsor must choose the single figure from the range, based on a conversation about understanding, assumptions and risk', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 2 is true', explain: 'AgilePM recommends ranges. A demanded single figure is the Sponsor\'s choice, not the estimator\'s, after an explicit risk conversation.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'final28',
    title: 'Final Paper 28',
    questions: [
      { id: 'F28Q1', topic: 'KC', q: 'Which 1994 response to both Waterfall and RAD later evolved into AgilePM?', options: ['Scientific Management', 'The Cone of Uncertainty diagram', 'Planning Poker', 'DSDM'], correct: 'DSDM', explain: 'The DSDM Consortium formed in 1994. AgilePM was released in 2010; the Consortium rebranded as the Agile Business Consortium in 2016.' },
      { id: 'F28Q2', topic: 'KC', q: 'Identify the missing outcome. In extreme applications of Taylorism, employees were treated like [ ? ].', options: ['empowered decision-makers', 'self-managing Developers', 'Product Owners', 'machines'], correct: 'machines', explain: 'Staff followed procedures as system operators and were not expected to understand the reasoning. Extremes treated people like machines — the opposite of agility.' },
      { id: 'F28Q3', topic: 'KC', q: 'Which of the following correctly describe latent value and why projects exist?\n1. Latent value is potential product value realised only when the product is in use\n2. Projects are used to provide focus and coordination and to control investment and resources\n3. Latent value is realised the moment a PBI is written\n4. A solution-focused view helps ensure latent value is captured', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'Writing a backlog item does not realise value. Use and the wider solution (including change) do.' },
      { id: 'F28Q4', topic: 'KC', q: 'Which statement best captures how Scrum and AgilePM differ on adaptability?', options: ['Both forbid any tailoring of roles or events', 'AgilePM freezes its lifecycle; Scrum invites each team to invent new accountabilities', 'Only Taylorist organisations may tailor AgilePM', 'Scrum\'s roles, artefacts, events and rules are treated as unchangeable; AgilePM\'s guidance is expected to be tailored to the organisation'], correct: 'Scrum\'s roles, artefacts, events and rules are treated as unchangeable; AgilePM\'s guidance is expected to be tailored to the organisation', explain: 'Scrum is effective only when its core is intact. AgilePM is a framework of guidance designed to be configured to context, including via the PAQ.' },
      { id: 'F28Q5', topic: 'KC', q: 'Consider the statements about the last responsible moment.\n1. Detailed analysis, design and planning are completed when the information is actually needed for the next step, not as far in advance as possible\n2. Completing detailed work at that moment is more efficient because near-future predictions are more reliable and later decisions benefit from what has been learned', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Last responsible moment reduces wasted documentation and improves decisions. It is not an excuse to avoid Foundations-level understanding.' },
      { id: 'F28Q6', topic: 'SC', q: 'Identify the missing maximum. The Sprint itself is a fixed-length event of [ ? ] or less, and a new Sprint starts immediately after the previous one ends.', options: ['one week', 'one quarter', 'one year', 'one month'], correct: 'one month', explain: 'Sprints are one month or less. Quality does not decrease during the Sprint and changes that endanger the Sprint Goal are not allowed.' },
      { id: 'F28Q7', topic: 'SC', q: 'Which of the following are Developer accountabilities in Scrum?\n1. Creating a plan for the Sprint (the Sprint Backlog)\n2. Instilling quality by adhering to the Definition of Done\n3. Owning the Business Case and project budget\n4. Adapting their plan each day toward the Sprint Goal', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'Developers plan the Sprint, uphold the DoD, adapt daily and hold each other accountable. They do not own the Business Case or budget.' },
      { id: 'F28Q8', topic: 'SC', q: 'What happens if a Product Backlog item does not meet the Definition of Done at the end of the Sprint?', options: ['It may still be presented at the Sprint Review as Done', 'It is automatically recategorised as a Won\'t Have', 'The Scrum Master marks it 90% complete for governance', 'It cannot be released or presented at the Sprint Review and returns to the Product Backlog'], correct: 'It cannot be released or presented at the Sprint Review and returns to the Product Backlog', explain: 'Done means the DoD is met. Percentage-complete reporting is not a substitute for a usable Increment.' },
      { id: 'F28Q9', topic: 'SC', q: 'Consider the statements about Sprint Review and Retrospective timeboxes for a one-month Sprint.\n1. The Sprint Review is timeboxed to a maximum of 4 hours and should be a working session, not merely a presentation\n2. The Sprint Retrospective is timeboxed to a maximum of 3 hours', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Review inspects the outcome and future adaptations (max 4h). Retro inspects people, process, tools and DoD (max 3h).' },
      { id: 'F28Q10', topic: 'SC', q: 'The Product Backlog is bound to which commitment?', options: ['Sprint Goal', 'Definition of Done', 'Feasibility Assessment', 'Product Goal'], correct: 'Product Goal', explain: 'Each Scrum artefact has one commitment: Product Backlog → Product Goal; Sprint Backlog → Sprint Goal; Increment → Definition of Done.' },
      { id: 'F28Q11', topic: 'SC', q: 'Typical Scrum Team size is described as:', options: ['Exactly 15, including a steering committee', 'At least 25 so that specialists never collaborate', 'One Product Owner plus any number of Project Managers', '10 or fewer people — small enough to stay nimble, large enough to complete significant work'], correct: '10 or fewer people — small enough to stay nimble, large enough to complete significant work', explain: 'The Scrum Team is one SM, one PO and Developers, with no sub-teams or hierarchies, focused on one Product Goal at a time.' },
      { id: 'F28Q12', topic: 'AF', q: 'Identify the missing principle. [ ? ] means working closely with customer and stakeholder to improve the solution from operational feedback using short cycles, with frequent demonstrations, reviews, adjustments and an embrace of change.', options: ['Focus on the Business Need', 'Deliver on Time', 'Never Compromise Quality', 'Develop Iteratively'], correct: 'Develop Iteratively', explain: 'Principle 6 is iterative development. Incremental delivery from firm foundations is Principle 5 and is not the same idea.' },
      { id: 'F28Q13', topic: 'AF', q: 'The principles are enabled by five named practices. Which items belong in that set?\n1. Iterative Development\n2. Timeboxing (including Sprints)\n3. MoSCoW Prioritisation\n4. Fixed-scope legal discovery', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'The five practices are Iterative Development, Timeboxing, MoSCoW, Modelling and Facilitated Workshops.' },
      { id: 'F28Q14', topic: 'AF', q: 'Who is responsible for setting the Definition of Done for iterations and for setting and monitoring quality-assurance standards in AgilePM?', options: ['Business Sponsor', 'Process Advisor acting alone', 'External auditor with no project role', 'Solution Architect'], correct: 'Solution Architect', explain: 'The Solution Architect sets a realistic DoD and QA standards. Quality oversight also involves BV, PO, regulators and others, with the PM ensuring it happens.' },
      { id: 'F28Q15', topic: 'AF', q: 'Consider the statements about Parkinson\'s Law in the guide.\n1. Parkinson\'s Law is that work expands to fill the time available\n2. It is one reason sponsors hesitate to set aside formal time or budget contingency', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Traditional contingency is hard to protect. AgilePM therefore puts contingency in feature scope via MoSCoW.' },
      { id: 'F28Q16', topic: 'AF', q: 'Before major development starts, Principle 5 wants a high-level grasp of which trio?', options: ['The coffee-card estimate, the email standard and the Gantt baseline', 'Only the Sprint Backlog tasks for day one', 'The Benefits Assessment after three years of operation', 'The business problem, the proposed solution, and associated risk'], correct: 'The business problem, the proposed solution, and associated risk', explain: 'Firm foundations mean understanding problem, solution and risk before major development — not detailing every requirement.' },
      { id: 'F28Q17', topic: 'IN', q: 'Which of the following sit in the Value Ownership domain?\n1. Business Sponsor\n2. Business Visionary\n3. Product Owner, from a Scrum perspective\n4. Project Manager', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'Value Ownership is Business Sponsor and Business Visionary. The Product Owner is the Scrum role in that domain. The Project Manager sits in Delivery Enablement.' },
      { id: 'F28Q18', topic: 'IN', q: 'Identify the missing focus. Agile Leadership focuses on maximising transparency of the evolving problem and solution, and on clearly defining [ ? ] so it is obvious who may decide in each circumstance.', options: ['a single Gantt owner', 'the coffee-cup card', 'a blame clause', 'role accountabilities'], correct: 'role accountabilities', explain: 'Agile leaders share an evolving understanding of the problem/solution and define who has authority to decide. They develop new leaders, not followers.' },
      { id: 'F28Q19', topic: 'IN', q: 'The Business Visionary may delegate Product Backlog work to others. What remains true after that delegation?', options: ['Accountability moves to the Developers', 'The Scrum Master becomes Accountable for the Product Backlog', 'No one remains accountable', 'The Business Visionary remains accountable'], correct: 'The Business Visionary remains accountable', explain: 'Responsibility for backlog work can be delegated (typically to Product Owner(s)), but the Business Visionary remains accountable.' },
      { id: 'F28Q20', topic: 'IN', q: 'Consider the statements about true leadership.\n1. The Scrum Guide defines success for a true leader by the effectiveness of their Delivery Team(s)\n2. Success comes from developing others and helping them be as effective as a team as they can be', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'True leadership is servant leadership measured by team effectiveness, not by personal control of tasks.' },
      { id: 'F28Q21', topic: 'IN', q: 'Figure 5.7-style PM relationships include which of the following?\n1. Business Sponsor\n2. Business Visionary\n3. Solution Architect\n4. External payroll bureau with no project interest', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'The PM\'s six key relationships include BS, BV, SA, Product Owner, Scrum Master and other stakeholders. A disinterested payroll bureau is not one of them.' },
      { id: 'F28Q22', topic: 'IN', q: 'Stakeholder-related issues and risks are owned by which pairing, with the Project Manager also supporting communication to governance authorities?', options: ['The Process Advisor alone', 'The Daily Scrum facilitator only', 'The Project Manager and the Product Owner', 'Whoever last updated the burn-down chart'], correct: 'The Project Manager and the Product Owner', explain: 'PM and PO own stakeholder issues and risks. The PM supports timely communication with governance authorities and others not in the project day-to-day.' },
      { id: 'F28Q23', topic: 'IN', q: 'Decision rights for the Solution Architect cover which area?', options: ['How the project is managed day to day', 'Business-value-related decisions reserved to the Visionary', 'The integrity and delivery of the solution', 'Whether the Business Sponsor may hold a budget'], correct: 'The integrity and delivery of the solution', explain: 'BV decides business value; SA decides solution integrity and delivery; PM decides how the project is managed. The three senior roles share equal authority.' },
      { id: 'F28Q24', topic: 'CC', q: 'In the Highsmith line used for communication and governance, which word completes: documentation is not understanding, [ ? ] is not discipline, formality is not skill?', options: ['velocity', 'MoSCoW', 'process', 'empiricism'], correct: 'process', explain: 'The Highsmith line is used for communication and for agile governance: paperwork and formality are not substitutes for understanding, discipline or skill.' },
      { id: 'F28Q25', topic: 'CC', q: 'Which of the following correctly describe Team Collaboration?\n1. Members understand each other\'s skills and availability toward a common goal\n2. The team shares responsibility for difficulties and celebrates success equally\n3. Each person still commits only to individual recognition over team success\n4. People contribute differently based on expertise but support each other when needed', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'Collaboration requires respecting contributions and promoting team success over individual recognition. Statement 3 reverses that.' },
      { id: 'F28Q26', topic: 'CC', q: 'Community Collaboration in AgilePM is best described as focusing on:', options: ['Replacing the Product Owner with a steering pack', 'Banning Facilitated Workshops', 'A sense of unity among all teams and individuals creating and delivering value, including knowledge-sharing across organisational boundaries', 'Keeping high-power stakeholders uninformed'], correct: 'A sense of unity among all teams and individuals creating and delivering value, including knowledge-sharing across organisational boundaries', explain: 'Community collaboration applies the nine leadership principles and recognises the whole project community, not only one Scrum Team.' },
      { id: 'F28Q27', topic: 'CC', q: 'Consider the statements about over-using technology.\n1. Replacing face-to-face discussion with email or chat can cause misunderstanding because body language and tone are missing\n2. AgilePM therefore forbids all virtual meetings and collaborative workspaces', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 1 is true', explain: 'Technology can reduce effectiveness if overused, but virtual meetings and digital workspaces are valid choices when used well.' },
      { id: 'F28Q28', topic: 'PR', q: 'Which of the following correctly describe increment relationships?\n1. Delivery Teams usually deliver one or more Product Increments per Sprint\n2. A Project Increment is the same thing as a single Daily Scrum\n3. Product Increments can contribute to a Solution Increment\n4. A Deployed Solution is created when a Solution Increment is deployed', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'Product → Solution → Deployed Solution. A Project Increment is a time horizon bound by Foundations and an increment review, not a 15-minute event.' },
      { id: 'F28Q29', topic: 'PR', q: 'Identify the missing artifact. The [ ? ] is an Optional evolutionary design framework covering business and technical aspects clearly enough to define solution scope without freezing every detail.', options: ['Sprint Backlog', 'Benefits Assessment', 'Solution Architecture Definition', 'Terms of Reference'], correct: 'Solution Architecture Definition', explain: 'SAD is Optional and evolutionary. Responsible Solution Architect, Accountable Business Visionary. NFRs often live here; late NFRs can be hard or impossible to add.' },
      { id: 'F28Q30', topic: 'PR', q: 'In a continuous-delivery lifecycle configuration (example 4), planning of the next Project Increment is ongoing via:', options: ['A mandatory multi-month return to Pre-Project after every Sprint', 'Cancelling Scrum until the auditor signs a Gantt chart', 'Cross-team refinement and Project Sprint Reviews, without a firebreak return to Foundations', 'Freezing the Product Backlog at Feasibility'], correct: 'Cross-team refinement and Project Sprint Reviews, without a firebreak return to Foundations', explain: 'Example 3 uses a firebreak back to Foundations. Example 4 keeps deploying within Sprints and plans the next increment through CTR and project reviews.' },
      { id: 'F28Q31', topic: 'PR', q: 'Consider the statements about Foundations duration and detail.\n1. Fine details are left for the Delivery Phase\n2. Foundations should last only a few weeks even for large projects, staying high-level enough to avoid delay', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Foundations builds a shared, lasting foundation (value, solution, process) and a high-level Delivery Plan — not a complete detailed design.' },
      { id: 'F28Q32', topic: 'PR', q: 'Who is typically involved as Project Roles during Pre-Project?', options: ['Only Developers estimating story points', 'Only an external auditor', 'Business Visionary defining the vision and Business Sponsor providing funding and support', 'The entire operational user population'], correct: 'Business Visionary defining the vision and Business Sponsor providing funding and support', explain: 'Pre-Project can be a short conversation or a formal proposal. The two key roles are BV (vision) and BS (funding/support).' },
      { id: 'F28Q33', topic: 'ES', q: 'Identify the missing test. A practical check for a true Must Have is: would the project be [ ? ] if this requirement were not met?', options: ['cancelled', 'praised in the press', 'given extra Could Haves', 'excused from a Definition of Done'], correct: 'cancelled', explain: 'If the answer is no, it is not a Must Have. Other tests include legal/safety need, no viable workaround, and no dependence on a Should or Could.' },
      { id: 'F28Q34', topic: 'ES', q: 'Which of the following are true of the Minimum Useable Subset?\n1. It is made up of the Must Have requirements\n2. It includes every Could Have on the first Sprint\n3. It must be delivered within the timebox for the project to succeed\n4. It is the set that is guaranteed if MoSCoW is applied correctly', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'MUST = Must Haves. Could Haves are contingency, not part of the guaranteed subset.' },
      { id: 'F28Q35', topic: 'ES', q: 'During Feasibility, high-level functional requirements are usually expressed as:', options: ['Epic stories outlining broad goals', 'Fine-grained Sprint tasks of one hour', 'Signed source-code comments', 'A completed Benefits Assessment'], correct: 'Epic stories outlining broad goals', explain: 'Feasibility uses a small number of epics, clear enough to define scope, test feasibility and range costs/benefits. Fine-grained stories come later via refinement.' },
      { id: 'F28Q36', topic: 'ES', q: 'Which of the following are true about MoSCoW and the Business Case?\n1. Worst-case benefits are based on the Must Haves only\n2. Expected-case benefits are based on Must Haves plus Should Haves\n3. Cost estimates should cover only the Must Haves\n4. The Business Visionary is responsible for ensuring priorities achieve the expected RoI', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'Costs cover full in-scope work. Benefits are framed as worst/expected/best using Must / Must+Should / Must+Should+Could.' },
      { id: 'F28Q37', topic: 'ES', q: 'Consider the statements about Acceptance Criteria and the Definition of Done.\n1. Meeting Acceptance Criteria is usually one criterion within the Definition of Done, not a replacement for it\n2. Acceptance Criteria and the Definition of Done are identical lists and either term may be used for the other', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 1 is true', explain: 'AC say what this item must satisfy. DoD is the shared quality bar for every Increment. They are related, not interchangeable.' },
      { id: 'F28Q38', topic: 'ES', q: 'If the project is high risk (new technology or an inexperienced team), what should happen to the usual Must Have effort share?', options: ['Reduce Must Have effort below the usual level to leave more contingency', 'Increase Must Haves above 80% to force delivery', 'Convert all Should Haves into Must Haves', 'Stop using timeboxes'], correct: 'Reduce Must Have effort below the usual level to leave more contingency', explain: 'The 60/20 guidance is a starting balance, not a law. Higher risk means fewer Must Haves so the buffer can absorb uncertainty.' },
      { id: 'F28Q39', topic: 'PC', q: 'Identify the missing concept. [ ? ] means tracking progress by delivered value, not merely by tasks completed.', options: ['Outcome-based measurement', 'Percentage-complete accounting', 'Taylorist time-and-motion study', 'Contractual blame allocation'], correct: 'Outcome-based measurement', explain: 'An Increment is an output. The outcome appears when the product or solution is used. That is why Realisation and Benefits Assessment matter.' },
      { id: 'F28Q40', topic: 'PC', q: 'Advance Deployment planning should lock in which of the following?\n1. The Product Owner is removed so operations can improvise\n2. The right resources (people, tools, facilities) are available\n3. Necessary business-change activities such as training are scheduled\n4. A rollback option exists if major problems arise after go-live, or a Plan B if rollback is impossible', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '2, 3, 4', explain: 'Deployment is more than technical release. The Delivery Plan is updated as deployment work becomes clear, often via Cross-Team Refinement.' },
      { id: 'F28Q41', topic: 'PC', q: 'Accountability for wider business change and later benefits realisation sits with which role?', options: ['Business Visionary', 'Scrum Master', 'A Developer chosen at the Daily Scrum', 'The Process Advisor'], correct: 'Business Visionary', explain: 'The PM makes sure realisation activities are planned. The Business Visionary is accountable for the wider business change and benefits realisation.' },
      { id: 'F28Q42', topic: 'PC', q: 'Consider the statements about continuous deployment during a Sprint.\n1. Deployments of Done Product Increments may occur during a Sprint if they align with the Delivery Plan and do not harm other teams or products\n2. A Delivery Team may deploy unilaterally at any time without planning the deployment in Sprint Planning', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 1 is true', explain: 'Continuous delivery is acceptable when planned and aligned. Unilateral deployment that surprises other teams is not.' },
      { id: 'F28Q43', topic: 'PC', q: 'A Scrum of Scrums is primarily used to:', options: ['Replace the Product Owner', 'Let representatives share updates and manage cross-team dependencies', 'Approve the Business Case', 'Set Must Have percentages for finance'], correct: 'Let representatives share updates and manage cross-team dependencies', explain: 'SoS attendees vary (Developers for a dependency, Scrum Masters for progress/impediments). It is not the only time teams may collaborate.' },
      { id: 'F28Q44', topic: 'RK', q: 'Identify the missing number. The Project Approach Questionnaire contains [ ? ] statements rated from Strongly Agree to Strongly Disagree.', options: ['8', '17', '12', '60'], correct: '17', explain: 'The PAQ has 17 consensus statements. Any that are not true indicate approach-based risk to be tailored and mitigated.' },
      { id: 'F28Q45', topic: 'RK', q: 'Which of the following are true of how AgilePM handles VUCA-related risk?\n1. A separate risk register always replaces the need to apply the principles\n2. It uses agile philosophy, principles and practices to guide value delivery\n3. How fully the guidance is followed influences the approach\'s intrinsic ability to deal with risk\n4. Quality, risk and governance are considered from the outset, including Feasibility and Foundations workshops', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '2, 3, 4', explain: 'Risk is a consequence of VUCA and is managed in the day-to-day approach. A separate process is the exception, not the default replacement.' },
      { id: 'F28Q46', topic: 'RK', q: 'If Delivery Team members can only work part-time (PAQ Statement 13), which action does AgilePM recommend first?', options: ['Extend every Sprint to a quarter so part-timers can catch up', 'Agree the ways of working in the MAD and DAD and gain commitment to them, then plan Sprint Goals around real availability', 'Convert all requirements to Must Haves', 'Cancel the Daily Scrum'], correct: 'Agree the ways of working in the MAD and DAD and gain commitment to them, then plan Sprint Goals around real availability', explain: 'Do not extend Sprints as the default fix. Avoid overcommitting; if availability is highly unpredictable, prefer a flexible target date and escalate to the Sponsor if needed.' },
      { id: 'F28Q47', topic: 'GV', q: 'Identify the missing pairing. For the Benefits Assessment, Responsible is the [ ? ] and Accountable is the [ ? ].', options: ['Project Manager; Product Owner', 'Business Visionary; Business Sponsor', 'Developers; Scrum Master', 'Solution Architect; Process Advisor'], correct: 'Business Visionary; Business Sponsor', explain: 'Benefits Assessment is Recommended and evolutionary. It records how expected benefits have actually materialised after live use. R=BV, A=BS.' },
      { id: 'F28Q48', topic: 'GV', q: 'Which statements about the Feasibility Assessment and Foundations Summary are correct?\n1. Feasibility Assessment is an Optional milestone; Responsible Project Manager, Accountable Business Sponsor\n2. Foundations Summary is an Optional milestone; Responsible Project Manager, Accountable Business Sponsor\n3. Both are created only by the Scrum Master, who is Accountable for investment decisions\n4. Feasibility Assessment supports the decision on whether the project seems practical; Foundations Summary helps assess likely return on investment', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '2, 3, 4', explain: 'Both snapshots are R=PM A=BS. The SM is not accountable for investment decisions. The Sponsor keeps reviewing the Business Case and can stop the project.' },
      { id: 'F28Q49', topic: 'GV', q: 'Which AgilePM artifacts are specifically listed as customisable to show regulatory compliance?', options: ['Daily Scrum attendance list, coffee-cup cards and a private chat log', 'Solution Architecture Definition, Sprint Review Records and Project Review Reports', 'Only the Terms of Reference', 'Only Planning Poker results'], correct: 'Solution Architecture Definition, Sprint Review Records and Project Review Reports', explain: 'Those three are named. In regulated settings the Sprint Review Record may need to be formal and auditable. Keep records accurate, signed and stored, but still lightweight.' },
      { id: 'F28Q50', topic: 'GV', q: 'Consider the statements about money-related Project Artifacts used at different times.\n1. The Feasibility Assessment gives an early view of whether the project is likely to make business sense\n2. Benefits Assessment measures real, observed benefits after the solution has been used in the real world for a while', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Business Case explains value and cost in more detail. Foundations Summary is a shorter form. Project Review Report shows which benefits should now start to appear at each release.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'final29',
    title: 'Final Paper 29',
    questions: [
      { id: 'F29Q1', topic: 'KC', q: 'The DSDM Consortium was formed in 1994 and later rebranded. In which year did it become the Agile Business Consortium?', options: ['1991', '2001', '2010', '2016'], correct: '2016', explain: 'RAD 1991, DSDM Consortium 1994, Manifesto 2001, AgilePM 2010, ABC rebrand 2016. AgilePM v3 then blends that lineage with Scrum.' },
      { id: 'F29Q2', topic: 'KC', q: 'Agility requires people to be able to:', options: ['Follow Taylorist procedures without understanding them', 'Produce comprehensive documents before any experiment', 'Avoid collaboration until Deployment', 'Think, assess and adapt; move through complex situations; take effective and appropriate action'], correct: 'Think, assess and adapt; move through complex situations; take effective and appropriate action', explain: 'VUCA is handled by flexible, adaptable, thoughtful, collaborative problem-solvers — not by treating people as machine operators.' },
      { id: 'F29Q3', topic: 'KC', q: 'Identify the missing contrast. Scrum is often less suited where work is [ ? ], already well defined and highly repetitive, such as some manufacturing or distribution tasks.', options: ['complex and collaborative', 'research-like', 'emergent', 'standardised'], correct: 'standardised', explain: 'Scrum fits complex work where the solution is not fully known. Routine, well-defined, repetitive work is a poor fit.' },
      { id: 'F29Q4', topic: 'KC', q: 'Which of the following correctly describe the Manifesto\'s final sentence and ABC wording?\n1. Items on the right still have value, but items on the left are valued more\n2. The Consortium replaced software with solutions so the values apply beyond software\n3. Items on the right must never be produced\n4. Right-hand items are useful only when they add real value and contain just enough detail', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'The final sentence prevents a false dichotomy. Documentation, contracts, plans and tools remain useful when they serve value.' },
      { id: 'F29Q5', topic: 'KC', q: 'Consider the statements about product management agility in stable work.\n1. Even in relatively stable projects, product-management agility can still improve communication, involvement, results and incremental benefit delivery\n2. Agility is valuable only when every VUCA factor is at its maximum', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 1 is true', explain: 'The guide argues agility still helps in stable settings. It is not reserved for extreme chaos.' },
      { id: 'F29Q6', topic: 'SC', q: 'How does the Scrum Master serve the organisation?', options: ['By directing Developers\' daily task assignment', 'By owning the Product Backlog', 'By choosing the Sponsor\'s single-figure estimate', 'By leading, training and coaching Scrum adoption and helping people enact empirical approaches'], correct: 'By leading, training and coaching Scrum adoption and helping people enact empirical approaches', explain: 'The SM also plans/advises implementations and removes barriers between stakeholders and teams. They do not become PO or estimator-of-record.' },
      { id: 'F29Q7', topic: 'SC', q: 'How does AgilePM describe the ongoing work of adding description, order and size to backlog items?', options: ['A one-off Foundations workshop that freezes every item', 'A substitute for the Sprint Retrospective', 'The Project Manager\'s private estimate log', 'The ongoing act of breaking down and further defining items, adding details such as description, order and size'], correct: 'The ongoing act of breaking down and further defining items, adding details such as description, order and size', explain: 'Refinement is continuous. Items are ready for Sprint Planning when they can be completed in a single Sprint.' },
      { id: 'F29Q8', topic: 'SC', q: 'Identify the missing duration. During Topic Three of Sprint Planning, selected items are often decomposed into work items of [ ? ] or less.', options: ['one month', 'one Project Increment', 'one financial year', 'one day'], correct: 'one day', explain: 'Topic Three is how the work will get done. Decomposition to about a day or less makes the plan inspectable in Daily Scrums.' },
      { id: 'F29Q9', topic: 'SC', q: 'A Scrum Team sits down to plan a Sprint. Which items are legitimate inputs to that event?\n1. Product Backlog\n2. The latest Product Increment\n3. Past performance of the Scrum Team\n4. The finished Sprint Backlog from the Sprint that has not yet been planned', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'The Sprint Backlog is an output of Planning, not an input to the same Planning event.' },
      { id: 'F29Q10', topic: 'SC', q: 'Consider the statements about the Product Goal.\n1. The Product Goal describes a future state of the product that the Scrum Team plans against\n2. A new Product Goal may be taken on before the current one is fulfilled or abandoned', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 1 is true', explain: 'The current Product Goal must be fulfilled or abandoned before the next one is taken on. The Product Backlog is the single source of work toward that goal.' },
      { id: 'F29Q11', topic: 'SC', q: 'Inspection without adaptation is considered:', options: ['A complete governance success', 'The same as the Product Goal', 'Mandatory in Pre-Project only', 'Pointless'], correct: 'Pointless', explain: 'The three pillars chain together: transparency enables inspection; inspection enables adaptation. Skipping adaptation wastes the inspection.' },
      { id: 'F29Q12', topic: 'AF', q: 'What does empowerment require people to be, in the Manifesto discussion used by AgilePM?', options: ['Silent, interchangeable and uninformed', 'Authorised only to escalate', 'Replaced by procedures', 'Capable, willing and informed'], correct: 'Capable, willing and informed', explain: 'Capable (skills), willing (ready to take responsibility) and informed (enough data). Boundaries of empowerment then state how far they may decide without extra approval.' },
      { id: 'F29Q13', topic: 'AF', q: 'Identify the missing culture. Principle 3 (Collaborate) requires building a [ ? ] culture focused on the success of the entire Project Organisation.', options: ['hero-developer', 'audit-only', 'sponsor-versus-team', 'one-team'], correct: 'one-team', explain: 'The PM involves the right stakeholders, encourages business participation and ensures people are empowered to decide. Success is organisational, not local.' },
      { id: 'F29Q14', topic: 'AF', q: 'Which of the following are true of Never Compromise Quality?\n1. Expected quality is defined at the beginning of the project\n2. The solution should be fit for purpose — good enough for the business need, not exceeding or falling short as a habit\n3. Quality may be dropped first whenever a Sprint feels busy\n4. Business Visionary, Solution Architect, Product Owner and appropriate regulatory stakeholders provide quality oversight', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'Quality is a fixed variable. Features flex; the DoD protects quality from becoming the accidental variable.' },
      { id: 'F29Q15', topic: 'AF', q: 'Consider the statements about reports and Demonstrate Control.\n1. Reports should not be relied on as the main governance mechanism because they can be outdated and miss what is visible in the real work\n2. Progress should be tracked on working solutions rather than completed activities alone', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Demonstrate Control means visible work, clear accountabilities, agreed process and actual progress — not theatre-of-reporting.' },
      { id: 'F29Q16', topic: 'AF', q: 'In what way does the wider AgilePM context support a Product Owner\'s decisions?', options: ['By removing them from stakeholder conversations', 'By letting the Project Manager rewrite the Product Backlog nightly', 'By forbidding a Definition of Done', 'By providing business, user and corporate context so product decisions stay aligned with overall goals'], correct: 'By providing business, user and corporate context so product decisions stay aligned with overall goals', explain: 'AgilePM wraps Scrum product work in project vision, Business Case, architecture and multi-team structure so the PO is not deciding in a vacuum.' },
      { id: 'F29Q17', topic: 'IN', q: 'Which leadership-spectrum ends does AgilePM describe, noting that most agile frameworks encourage empowerment rather than a single "correct" style?', options: ['Taylorism and Scientific Management only', 'Email and instant message', 'Must Have and Won\'t Have', 'Servant leadership and command-and-control'], correct: 'Servant leadership and command-and-control', explain: 'Styles sit on a spectrum from "do whatever you think best" to "do exactly as I say". AgilePM and Scrum encourage empowering self-organisation.' },
      { id: 'F29Q18', topic: 'IN', q: 'The Business Visionary\'s decision rights concern which class of decisions?', options: ['How the project is managed as a control system', 'The Fibonacci scale on Planning Poker cards', 'Whether Scrum events may exist', 'Business-value-related decisions'], correct: 'Business-value-related decisions', explain: 'Equal-authority split: BV = business value; SA = solution integrity/delivery; PM = how the project is managed. BS empowers the others.' },
      { id: 'F29Q19', topic: 'IN', q: 'Identify the missing pairing. A Workshop Facilitator is given as an example of a [ ? ] Advisor.', options: ['Business', 'Solution', 'Process', 'Financial'], correct: 'Process', explain: 'Process Advisors (for example Agile Coach or Workshop Facilitator) help the organisation enablement side tailor and apply practices. They do not take Value Ownership.' },
      { id: 'F29Q20', topic: 'IN', q: 'Which of the following are Solution Architect accountabilities?\n1. Owning the solution architecture and delivery approach\n2. Ensuring regulatory or quality standards are reflected in the Definition of Done\n3. Holding the project budget as Project Champion\n4. Providing guidance across Delivery Teams so the solution stays coherent', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'Budget and Project Champion duties belong to the Business Sponsor. The SA also owns solution-delivery risk.' },
      { id: 'F29Q21', topic: 'IN', q: 'Consider the statements about roles and people.\n1. Roles do not necessarily equate to people; one person may fulfil more than one role\n2. A single role may be shared between multiple people if necessary', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Flexible mapping is normal, especially on small projects (for example a Solution Architect who also develops). Accountabilities must still be clear.' },
      { id: 'F29Q22', topic: 'IN', q: 'Developers in AgilePM are:', options: ['Only programmers, by job title', 'Forbidden from estimating', 'Anyone in the Scrum Team working Sprint Backlog tasks to help build a usable Product Increment; the title simplifies, it does not exclude', 'Accountable for the Terms of Reference'], correct: 'Anyone in the Scrum Team working Sprint Backlog tasks to help build a usable Product Increment; the title simplifies, it does not exclude', explain: 'Cross-functional makeup is intentional. "Developer" is used to simplify, not to exclude analysts, testers or other skills.' },
      { id: 'F29Q23', topic: 'IN', q: 'Who should manage relationships with external stakeholders and coordinate decision-making, facilitated by the Project Manager?', options: ['Developers only, during the Daily Scrum', 'The Benefits Assessment template', 'Business Visionary and Solution Architect', 'Whoever holds the coffee-cup card'], correct: 'Business Visionary and Solution Architect', explain: 'BV and SA manage those relationships. Purpose, timing, authority and constraints shape how external people engage.' },
      { id: 'F29Q24', topic: 'CC', q: 'Collaboration and communication map most directly onto which pair of AgilePM Principles?', options: ['Focus on the Business Need and Deliver on Time', 'Never Compromise Quality and Demonstrate Control', 'Collaborate and Communicate Continuously and Clearly', 'Build Incrementally from Firm Foundations and Develop Iteratively'], correct: 'Collaborate and Communicate Continuously and Clearly', explain: 'Principles 3 and 7 are the collaboration/communication pair. The others support them but are not the direct pair.' },
      { id: 'F29Q25', topic: 'CC', q: 'What can over-using email as the primary channel lead to, according to AgilePM?', options: ['Richer body language than a workshop', 'Automatic compliance with every regulation', 'Misunderstanding, because tone and body language are absent; email is better used to confirm previous discussions', 'A replacement for the Product Goal'], correct: 'Misunderstanding, because tone and body language are absent; email is better used to confirm previous discussions', explain: 'Choose the channel for the situation. Email suits confirmation and broadcast, not complex collaborative design as the default.' },
      { id: 'F29Q26', topic: 'CC', q: 'Which of the following are communication choices described in AgilePM?\n1. Face-to-face communication\n2. Virtual meetings\n3. Collaborative workspaces\n4. Silent Taylorist instruction sheets as the only allowed channel', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'The six choices include face-to-face, virtual meetings, instant messaging, social platforms, email and collaborative workspaces.' },
      { id: 'F29Q27', topic: 'CC', q: 'Consider the statements about Facilitated Workshops.\n1. They have a workshop owner with a clear goal, selected participants with knowledge and authority, and an independent facilitator\n2. The facilitator is a Process Advisor who ensures the process, not the solution content, achieves the outcome', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Independence of the facilitator is the point: they run the process so participants can decide. Modelling is the companion practice for shared understanding.' },
      { id: 'F29Q28', topic: 'PR', q: 'Once a Solution Increment is placed into the operating environment, what does AgilePM call the result?', options: ['The Sprint Goal written on a board', 'The Terms of Reference title page', 'What a Solution Increment becomes, or adds to, when it is deployed into the operating environment', 'A synonym for the Daily Scrum'], correct: 'What a Solution Increment becomes, or adds to, when it is deployed into the operating environment', explain: 'Several Product Increments may form a Solution Increment. Deployment turns that into (or extends) the Deployed Solution. Realisation then checks value in use.' },
      { id: 'F29Q29', topic: 'PR', q: 'By the end of Foundations, incremental delivery should have been:', options: ['Confirmed; it is first explored in Feasibility and confirmed by the end of Foundations', 'Avoided until the final month', 'Delegated entirely to an auditor', 'Replaced by a single big-bang design freeze'], correct: 'Confirmed; it is first explored in Feasibility and confirmed by the end of Foundations', explain: 'Whether a solution can be delivered incrementally is a lifecycle question, not a Sprint tactic only. Confirm it before major Delivery commitment.' },
      { id: 'F29Q30', topic: 'PR', q: 'Identify the missing artifact status. The Delivery Plan is a [ ? ] evolutionary Project Artifact, Responsible and Accountable both the Project Manager.', options: ['Required', 'Forbidden', 'Identical to the Sprint Backlog', 'Owned by the Business Sponsor as Responsible'], correct: 'Required', explain: 'Delivery Plan R=PM A=PM. It shows increments and, in simple form, the Sprints of the next increment — not every Developer task.' },
      { id: 'F29Q31', topic: 'PR', q: 'Which of the following are Feasibility artifacts and their optionality?\n1. Business Case — Recommended\n2. Management Approach Definition — Recommended\n3. Solution Architecture Definition — Optional\n4. Sprint Backlog — Required in Feasibility', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'Feasibility may also outline Optional Solution Backlog and DAD. A Sprint Backlog belongs to Delivery, not Feasibility.' },
      { id: 'F29Q32', topic: 'PR', q: 'Consider the statements about subsequent passes through Foundations.\n1. Revisiting Foundations after a Project Increment keeps early planning flexible and avoids unnecessary complexity from VUCA\n2. From a PM perspective, later Foundations passes improve agility of working and governance effectiveness, both reflected in the MAD', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Just enough detail, just in time. Long-term detail waits until it is needed. Check viability, refine requirements and update the Delivery Plan between increments.' },
      { id: 'F29Q33', topic: 'ES', q: 'Non-functional requirements discovered too late are a particular danger because they:', options: ['May be difficult or even impossible to include if not refined into the Solution Architecture Definition in time', 'Automatically become Won\'t Haves', 'Only affect the Daily Scrum agenda', 'Replace the need for a Definition of Done'], correct: 'May be difficult or even impossible to include if not refined into the Solution Architecture Definition in time', explain: 'Capture NFRs early in outline SAD during Feasibility and refine them in Foundations. Late NFRs can break architecture.' },
      { id: 'F29Q34', topic: 'ES', q: 'Identify the missing C. The Three Cs of a User Story are Card, Conversation and [ ? ].', options: ['Confirmation', 'Contract', 'Contingency', 'Compliance'], correct: 'Confirmation', explain: 'Card, Conversation, Confirmation. Some practitioners add Context; Contract is a common trap. Confirmation is often expressed as Acceptance Criteria.' },
      { id: 'F29Q35', topic: 'ES', q: 'Which of the following are suggested Definition of Ready ideas?\n1. The item has a clear outcome and understood scope\n2. The item has already been released to all users\n3. Value is identified and aligned with the Product Goal\n4. Dependencies and risks are understood', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'DoR is a ready-for-Sprint checklist (also small enough, AC agreed, people/resources, estimated). Released-to-all-users is Done/deployed, not Ready.' },
      { id: 'F29Q36', topic: 'ES', q: 'Consider the statements about Velocity.\n1. Velocity is unique to each team and should not be used to compare teams\n2. It is a measure of output, not outcomes, and should not be the sole measure of delivery progress', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Velocity helps Sprint Planning Topic 2. Team-composition changes mean velocity must be re-established. It is not a value metric.' },
      { id: 'F29Q37', topic: 'ES', q: 'In Planning Poker, what is the Project Manager\'s appropriate role?', options: ['Observe to understand assumptions for later high-level planning, without estimating or influencing the team', 'Estimate alongside Developers and overrule outliers', 'Assign every card value in advance', 'Cancel the session if any estimate exceeds 5'], correct: 'Observe to understand assumptions for later high-level planning, without estimating or influencing the team', explain: 'The SM usually facilitates; Developers estimate; the PO clarifies. PM influence would weaken the team\'s ownership of the estimates.' },
      { id: 'F29Q38', topic: 'ES', q: 'Identify the missing style. Estimating by decomposition is also known as [ ? ] estimating.', options: ['bottom-up', 'top-down portfolio only', 'Taylorist piece-rate', 'single-figure sponsor'], correct: 'bottom-up', explain: 'Decomposition/bottom-up breaks work until effort is clear. Analogy/comparative groups similar sizes. Using both cross-checks uncertainty; decomposition alone does not remove it.' },
      { id: 'F29Q39', topic: 'PC', q: 'Under Management by Exception, a delivery-impacting issue that affects the wider project is escalated to whom?', options: ['The Project Manager, who then collaborates with the Product Owner and, if appropriate, the Business Visionary', 'Only the intern who drew the burn-up', 'The entire operational user base', 'The Planning Poker facilitator'], correct: 'The Project Manager, who then collaborates with the Product Owner and, if appropriate, the Business Visionary', explain: 'In basic Scrum the PO handles many exceptions. AgilePM adds the PM because issues often have project-wide impact. Other Project Roles join to stay aligned.' },
      { id: 'F29Q40', topic: 'PC', q: 'Identify the missing review timing. Unfinished requirements should be reviewed at least at the end of each Sprint and at the end of each [ ? ].', options: ['coffee break', 'Project Increment', 'financial century', 'unused email thread'], correct: 'Project Increment', explain: 'Priorities drift. Minimum reviews are end of Sprint and end of Project Increment so the backlog still matches the Business Case.' },
      { id: 'F29Q41', topic: 'PC', q: 'Which of the following are reasons to revisit Foundations between Project Increments?\n1. Freeze every future Sprint Goal for the rest of the year\n2. Confirm the project is still viable using actual progress and an updated Business Case view\n3. Refine high-level requirements for the next increment\n4. Update the Delivery Plan from lessons learned', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '2, 3, 4', explain: 'Do not lock a year of Sprint Goals in a VUCA setting. Also review the management approach and avoid raising the Must Have percentage when adding work.' },
      { id: 'F29Q42', topic: 'PC', q: 'A cumulative flow chart is used primarily to:', options: ['Rank Delivery Teams for bonuses', 'Track item status (not started, in progress, done) over time to reveal workflow and bottlenecks', 'Replace the Product Goal', 'Prove Taylorist piece-rates'], correct: 'Track item status (not started, in progress, done) over time to reveal workflow and bottlenecks', explain: 'Burn-down, burn-up and CFD support transparency, inspection and adaptation. They are not performance-comparison weapons.' },
      { id: 'F29Q43', topic: 'PC', q: 'Who plays a key role during a Sprint to keep the Product Increment and the Solution Increment aligned?', options: ['The Business Sponsor estimating story points', 'The Product Owner, collaborating with the Solution Architect', 'The auditor rewriting Acceptance Criteria nightly', 'Whoever holds the infinity card'], correct: 'The Product Owner, collaborating with the Solution Architect', explain: 'The PO guides the team so work yields a valuable Product Increment aligned to the Solution Increment and stakeholder value.' },
      { id: 'F29Q44', topic: 'RK', q: 'Risk in AgilePM is described as arising as a direct consequence of:', options: ['Using Facilitated Workshops', 'VUCA, so its management is integrated into day-to-day execution of the approach', 'Having a Definition of Done', 'Electing a Scrum Master'], correct: 'VUCA, so its management is integrated into day-to-day execution of the approach', explain: 'Internal or external events can threaten the Solution Goal. The approach itself is the risk method, except in highly regulated or safety contexts.' },
      { id: 'F29Q45', topic: 'RK', q: 'Which of the following match PAQ remedies?\n1. Statement 3 unclear vision — ask the Business Visionary to explain it and take questions\n2. Statement 12 missing technical skill — train, hire specialist help, or split work to keep it visible and sprint-sized\n3. Statement 17 standards in the way — ignore the regulation until after Deployment\n4. Statement 1 poor understanding of AgilePM/Scrum — train and brief, using accredited help if needed', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'For constraints, understand why the standard exists, which parts are flexible, involve Business Advisors, and build compliance into DoD and Acceptance Criteria. Do not ignore regulation.' },
      { id: 'F29Q46', topic: 'RK', q: 'Consider the statements about PAQ Statement 10 (empowerment).\n1. A framework of empowerment underpins both AgilePM and Scrum\n2. If the organisation will not support team empowerment, it cannot fully adopt AgilePM; strict top-down control is incompatible', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Constant approval from Project Roles slows iteration and risks dates. Transfer decision rights as the team proves it can deliver.' },
      { id: 'F29Q47', topic: 'GV', q: 'A (G) marker on an AgilePM artifact indicates that the artifact:', options: ['Must be green on the wall', 'May be used in governance activities such as gateways or proving corporate or regulatory standards', 'Is forbidden in regulated industries', 'Is always Optional'], correct: 'May be used in governance activities such as gateways or proving corporate or regulatory standards', explain: 'Colours show focus (value / solution / management). (G) means the same artifact can also serve governance. Formality must still not kill agility.' },
      { id: 'F29Q48', topic: 'GV', q: 'Identify the missing chooser. Early estimates sit inside the Cone (or Core) of Uncertainty associated with [ ? ]; they start vague and should narrow as the lifecycle progresses.', options: ['Frederick Winslow Taylor', 'Barry Boehm', 'James Grenning', 'Geoff Watts'], correct: 'Barry Boehm', explain: 'Boehm\'s cone/core shows accuracy improving from concept through Feasibility, Foundations, Delivery and Deployment. Give ranges, not false precision.' },
      { id: 'F29Q49', topic: 'GV', q: 'Which governance-document RACI lines match Appendix A?\n1. Terms of Reference — Responsible: Developers; Accountable: Scrum Master\n2. Business Case — Responsible: Business Visionary; Accountable: Business Sponsor\n3. Delivery Plan — Responsible: Project Manager; Accountable: Project Manager\n4. Project Review Report — Responsible: Project Manager; Accountable: Business Visionary', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '2, 3, 4', explain: 'ToR is R=Business Visionary A=Business Sponsor. It is a Recommended milestone used to justify Feasibility and to help portfolio prioritisation.' },
      { id: 'F29Q50', topic: 'GV', q: 'Consider the statements about assuring compliance in an agile way.\n1. The first step is to understand what the regulation or standard is trying to achieve, then meet that goal with agile practices\n2. In many organisations a qualified person involved as Business Advisor can support the SA, PO and Delivery Teams on interpretation and review of increments', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Two qualities matter: solution quality (meets the need, technically sound) and process quality (built lawfully, as intended, evidenced). Use agile transparency rather than a parallel paper factory.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'final30',
    title: 'Final Paper 30',
    questions: [
      { id: 'F30Q1', topic: 'KC', q: 'Identify the missing decade problem. DSDM was created because 1990s projects were often too big and slow, communication was poor, progress was measured as [ ? ] rather than value, and processes made change difficult.', options: ['working solutions delivered', 'latent value in use', 'inspect-and-adapt cycles', 'percentage complete'], correct: 'percentage complete', explain: 'Pre-DSDM failure modes included late, over-budget products that were the wrong thing, built without business involvement, and tracked as % complete.' },
      { id: 'F30Q2', topic: 'KC', q: 'Which people and systems cope best with VUCA, according to AgilePM?', options: ['Those that treat employees like machines and forbid questions', 'Those that freeze a detailed plan in Pre-Project', 'Those that value comprehensive documentation over working solutions', 'Those that are flexible, adaptable, thoughtful, collaborative and good at solving problems'], correct: 'Those that are flexible, adaptable, thoughtful, collaborative and good at solving problems', explain: 'Volatility, Uncertainty, Complexity and Ambiguity punish rigid operator models. Agility is a people capability, not a tool install.' },
      { id: 'F30Q3', topic: 'KC', q: 'Which of the following correctly describe RAD\'s weaknesses that DSDM set out to fix?\n1. Lack of a formalised specification, relying on talk, demos and short loops\n2. Difficulty supporting and scaling what was created\n3. Quick fixes that harmed quality because analysis and design were skipped\n4. Excessive upfront governance that forbade any user involvement', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'RAD had too much user-developer speed and too little control/quality. Waterfall had the opposite problem. DSDM aimed at both.' },
      { id: 'F30Q4', topic: 'KC', q: 'Consider the statements about Scrum versus AgilePM structure.\n1. For a team to be using Scrum effectively, the core roles, artefacts, events and rules must all be present and followed\n2. AgilePM expects the overall approach to be tailored; that adaptability is desirable, not a defect', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'This is the fixed-versus-tailored trap. Do not "tailor" Scrum by dropping events, and do not treat AgilePM as an unchangeable ritual script.' },
      { id: 'F30Q5', topic: 'KC', q: 'Product Lifecycle Management looks across ideation, development, distribution, servicing and retirement. How does it complement project management in AgilePM?', options: ['PLM replaces the need for a Business Case', 'They are mutually exclusive', 'PLM is only used in Taylorist factories', 'PM handles short-term objectives and structure; PLM keeps long-term product focus and feedback; AgilePM aims to deliver the right value within time and budget'], correct: 'PM handles short-term objectives and structure; PLM keeps long-term product focus and feedback; AgilePM aims to deliver the right value within time and budget', explain: 'Projects control investment for time-bound outcomes. Products live longer. Latent value is realised in use across that life, not at project closure paperwork.' },
      { id: 'F30Q6', topic: 'SC', q: 'Schwaber and Sutherland describe Scrum as lightweight, simple to understand, and:', options: ['Easy to master in a day', 'A replacement for all project governance', 'A Taylorist procedure manual', 'Difficult to master'], correct: 'Difficult to master', explain: 'The nutshell is: PO orders work; the team turns a selection into an Increment; inspect and adjust; repeat. Simple structure, hard practice.' },
      { id: 'F30Q7', topic: 'SC', q: 'Identify the missing event. The [ ? ] is described as the heartbeat of Scrum, where ideas are turned into value.', options: ['Feasibility Assessment', 'Terms of Reference workshop', 'Benefits Assessment', 'Sprint'], correct: 'Sprint', explain: 'All other Scrum events happen inside the Sprint. A new Sprint starts immediately after the previous one concludes.' },
      { id: 'F30Q8', topic: 'SC', q: 'Which of the following correctly describe Scrum Team design?\n1. One Scrum Master, one Product Owner and Developers\n2. No sub-teams or hierarchies inside the Scrum Team\n3. Focus on one Product Goal at a time\n4. A mandatory Project Manager inside every Scrum Team', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'The Project Manager is an AgilePM Project Role outside the Scrum Team definition. Scrum has no PM accountability inside the team.' },
      { id: 'F30Q9', topic: 'SC', q: 'Consider the statements about rules during the Sprint.\n1. No changes that would endanger the Sprint Goal; quality does not decrease\n2. Scope may be clarified or renegotiated with the Product Owner as more is learned', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'The Sprint is stable enough to protect the goal and quality, and flexible enough to refine scope with the PO. The Product Backlog is refined as needed.' },
      { id: 'F30Q10', topic: 'SC', q: 'To avoid confusion with Solution and Project Increments, AgilePM v3 renames the Scrum Guide Increment to:', options: ['Project Artifact', 'Work-product', 'Solution Goal', 'Product Increment'], correct: 'Product Increment', explain: 'Scrum says Increment; AgilePM says Product Increment so it can be distinguished from Solution and Project Increments. Work-products were renamed Project Artifacts.' },
      { id: 'F30Q11', topic: 'SC', q: 'The Sprint Review is the second-to-last event of the Sprint. What is the last event?', options: ['Sprint Planning', 'Daily Scrum', 'Project Pre-mortem', 'Sprint Retrospective'], correct: 'Sprint Retrospective', explain: 'Order: Planning, Daily Scrums, Review, Retro. The Retro plans ways to increase quality and effectiveness, including the DoD.' },
      { id: 'F30Q12', topic: 'AF', q: 'Identify the missing alignment target in the AgilePM philosophy: best value emerges when projects are aligned to [ ? ] while they deliver frequently with motivated, empowered collaborators.', options: ['the longest possible Gantt chart', 'a single Taylorist procedure', 'comprehensive unread documentation', 'clear goals'], correct: 'clear goals', explain: 'Every stakeholder should understand the vision, be empowered in their expertise, collaborate on a timely solution, and accept that understanding will change.' },
      { id: 'F30Q13', topic: 'AF', q: 'Under a waterfall-style triangle, which project variable is normally locked while timescale and budget absorb the overrun?', options: ['The Daily Scrum timebox', 'The number of Scrum Values', 'Latent value', 'Features'], correct: 'Features', explain: 'Waterfall fixes features and lets time/cost (and often quality) move. AgilePM reverses that: features flex, time/cost/quality do not.' },
      { id: 'F30Q14', topic: 'AF', q: 'Which of the following are true of Principle 2, Deliver on Time?\n1. A delayed Solution Increment can lose its value, especially with market or legal deadlines\n2. Value Ownership roles must always understand what will be delivered, when, and how much it will cost\n3. Artificial deadlines used only for control are encouraged as a motivational device\n4. On-time delivery of an acceptable solution is the default primary success measure when using fixed timeboxes', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'Artificial deadlines that ignore real need are harmful. PAQ Statement 4 reinforces on-time delivery of an acceptable solution as the primary measure.' },
      { id: 'F30Q15', topic: 'AF', q: 'Consider the statements about High-level plans under Demonstrate Control.\n1. High-level plans, designs and standards must define what needs to be done, how it will be achieved and when it will be delivered\n2. Transparency means making work visible, establishing accountabilities, following agreed processes and understanding actual progress', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'Control is demonstrated empirically, not by volume of unread status packs.' },
      { id: 'F30Q16', topic: 'AF', q: 'What extra support does AgilePM give a Scrum Master when dealing with business leaders and project managers?', options: ['By making the Scrum Master the budget holder', 'By instructing the SM to assign all Developer tasks', 'By removing Retrospectives', 'By giving a way to communicate with business leaders and project managers so teams get the support they need'], correct: 'By giving a way to communicate with business leaders and project managers so teams get the support they need', explain: 'The SM remains a true leader of team effectiveness. AgilePM supplies the project conversation the SM would otherwise lack.' },
      { id: 'F30Q17', topic: 'IN', q: 'Identify the missing leadership principle. [ ? ] is demonstrated by an active, visible leader who creates a vision for change and lends it credibility.', options: ['Emotion must be hidden from teams', 'Leadership is confined to the steering pack', 'Followers should not become leaders', 'Actions speak louder than words'], correct: 'Actions speak louder than words', explain: 'Visible, credible role-modelling is this principle. Agile leaders still develop other leaders rather than collecting followers.' },
      { id: 'F30Q18', topic: 'IN', q: 'Select the genuine Agile Leadership principles from the list below.\n1. Improved quality of thinking leads to improved outcomes\n2. Organisations improve through effective feedback\n3. Leaders devolve appropriate power and authority\n4. Command and control before collaboration', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 3', explain: 'Command-and-control-before-collaboration is not a leadership principle. Collaborative communities achieve more than individuals is another genuine principle.' },
      { id: 'F30Q19', topic: 'IN', q: 'The Product Owner accountabilities in a scaled AgilePM setting may be fulfilled by which alternative Value Ownership title?', options: ['Process Advisor', 'External regulator', 'Planning Poker dealer', 'Business Ambassador'], correct: 'Business Ambassador', explain: 'Value ownership may be a Business Ambassador. Changes to the Product Backlog still happen by influencing that owner, not by side-door edits.' },
      { id: 'F30Q20', topic: 'IN', q: 'Consider the statements about Business Analysts.\n1. AgilePM, like Scrum, may recognise a Business Analyst as a Developer in some organisations\n2. Analysts may work at strategic level (shaping strategy) and operational level (detailed acceptance criteria aligned to the DoD)', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'BA work bridges strategy and solution. They may heavily support backlog management without becoming a second Product Owner by stealth.' },
      { id: 'F30Q21', topic: 'IN', q: 'What do Solution Advisors typically provide?', options: ['The project budget', 'Cancellation of Sprints', 'Specialist, often technical, input on requirements, design and operational decisions, including integrity, testing, deployment and training of support staff', 'Ownership of the Terms of Reference'], correct: 'Specialist, often technical, input on requirements, design and operational decisions, including integrity, testing, deployment and training of support staff', explain: 'A Solution Advisor may later become a Developer. They collaborate with Solution Delivery roles, not take the Sponsor\'s accountabilities.' },
      { id: 'F30Q22', topic: 'IN', q: 'Which of the following correctly describe how multiple teams may scale?\n1. Teams may share one Product Backlog and one Product Goal\n2. Teams may pull from a shared Solution Backlog into separate Product Backlogs\n3. Each team must invent its own incompatible DoD and hide its backlog\n4. Cross-team refinement and a common Definition of Done support transparency', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'Shared Product Backlog teams work to a common DoD with the Solution Architect. Hiding work destroys empiricism at project scale.' },
      { id: 'F30Q23', topic: 'IN', q: 'The Business Sponsor is accountable for owning the Solution Backlog if it is used. Which other role may also be described as owning that backlog in the Visionary accountabilities?', options: ['Scrum Master only', 'A random Developer each Sprint', 'Business Visionary, who owns the Solution Backlog if used, plus the delivered solution and related business change', 'The Process Advisor'], correct: 'Business Visionary, who owns the Solution Backlog if used, plus the delivered solution and related business change', explain: 'Guide wording names both: Sponsor owns the Solution Backlog if used; Visionary also owns it if used, plus the evolving solution. RACI for Solution Backlog is R=BV A=BV. Treat BV as R and A; do not give RACI Responsible to the Sponsor.' },
      { id: 'F30Q24', topic: 'CC', q: 'Identify the missing Soft-skill cluster. Collaboration needs people who share purpose, respect contributions, help one another, adapt together, take initiative, and [ ? ].', options: ['compete for individual recognition only', 'withhold information until Deployment', 'trust teammates and uphold commitments', 'escalate every task'], correct: 'trust teammates and uphold commitments', explain: 'Those behaviours, plus Scrum Values (focus, commitment, openness, respect, courage), make collaboration possible. Communication is the carrier.' },
      { id: 'F30Q25', topic: 'CC', q: 'Which of the following are listed benefits of collaboration?\n1. Improve problem-solving\n2. Improve learning, including workplace learning and retrospectives\n3. Increase productivity over time\n4. Guarantee that no documentation is ever required', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 2, 4', explain: 'Cross-functional learning is the fourth listed benefit. Documents are still created when they add value or prove compliance.' },
      { id: 'F30Q26', topic: 'CC', q: 'A low-power, high-interest stakeholder sits in which Power/Interest treatment box?', options: ['Keep satisfied', 'Closely engage as if they hold the budget', 'Keep informed', 'Monitor with minimum effort'], correct: 'Keep informed', explain: 'Low power / high interest = keep informed. Matching style to power and interest avoids wasting access or creating resistance.' },
      { id: 'F30Q27', topic: 'CC', q: 'Consider the statements about modelling and workshops.\n1. Both practices are designed to prioritise people and communication over rigid tools, and working solutions over excess documents\n2. Signing a document eliminates risk in a VUCA world, so workshops are optional decoration', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 1 is true', explain: 'Signing does not eliminate VUCA risk. Workshops and models create shared understanding and commitment; documents record it only when useful.' },
      { id: 'F30Q28', topic: 'PR', q: 'Identify the missing phase purpose. [ ? ] makes sure the solution provides the predicted value; where deployment is ongoing, this work should also be ongoing and can continue after the project ends.', options: ['Pre-Project', 'Sprint Planning Topic Three', 'Realisation', 'Taylorist time study'], correct: 'Realisation', explain: 'Realisation is post-deployment value. The Business Visionary is accountable for embedding change; Benefits Assessment captures observed benefits.' },
      { id: 'F30Q29', topic: 'PR', q: 'Which of the following correctly describe lifecycle configuration examples?\n1. Example 1: one Project Increment, deploy after a series of Sprints\n2. Example 4: no Sprints are used at all\n3. Example 2: multiple Project Increments, deploy at the end of each\n4. Example 3: multiple deployments each Sprint, plus a firebreak back to Foundations', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'Example 4 still uses Sprints and may deploy many times per Sprint, but plans the next increment through CTR and project reviews rather than a Foundations firebreak.' },
      { id: 'F30Q30', topic: 'PR', q: 'What should the Business Case remain focused on during Foundations?', options: ['Every Developer task for the next year', 'The Sprint Backlog of Sprint one only', 'Purpose and justification — vision, cost-benefit and constraints — not a substitute project plan', 'A copy of the Daily Scrum notes'], correct: 'Purpose and justification — vision, cost-benefit and constraints — not a substitute project plan', explain: 'Specific requirements belong in architecture and backlogs. The Business Case stays evolutionary and high-level, refined in CTR or later Foundations.' },
      { id: 'F30Q31', topic: 'PR', q: 'Consider the statements about DAD in regulated work.\n1. By the end of the first Foundations, the delivery approach — including testing for products and the overall solution — should be agreed\n2. A regulated project may need a formally documented delivery approach with compliance mechanisms designed in', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'DAD is Optional/evolutionary, R=SA A=PM. Training on methods and tools for the next increment should be set up by the end of first Foundations.' },
      { id: 'F30Q32', topic: 'PR', q: 'Each Project Increment is concluded with a review of Product and Solution Increments. If there is no distinct Deployment Phase, that review can be done through:', options: ['Ongoing Cross-team Refinement and the Project Sprint Review', 'Ignoring increments until project close', 'A Taylorist time-and-motion study', 'The Business Sponsor estimating story points'], correct: 'Ongoing Cross-team Refinement and the Project Sprint Review', explain: 'The increment review is mandatory in spirit. The ceremony can fold into existing inspect-and-adapt events when deployment is continuous.' },
      { id: 'F30Q33', topic: 'ES', q: 'Identify the missing hierarchy. Organisational vision shapes Solution goals and backlogs, which shape Product goals and backlogs, which shape [ ? ] goals and backlogs contributing to Product Increments.', options: ['Taylor', 'Sprint', 'Portfolio-only', 'Audit'], correct: 'Sprint', explain: 'Nested backlogs and goals keep a PBI connected to solution and organisation intent. A Sprint Goal is a commitment, not a separate product strategy.' },
      { id: 'F30Q34', topic: 'ES', q: 'Which of the following are true of User Story levels through the lifecycle?\n1. Epics typically appear in Feasibility (often fewer than ten)\n2. Fine-grained tasks must all be written in Pre-Project\n3. User Stories are broken out in Foundations ahead of development\n4. Fine-grained stories appear as needed in refinement or Sprint Planning', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'Precision of estimates rises with understanding. Pre-Project only needs enough to justify investigating, not a decomposed Sprint plan.' },
      { id: 'F30Q35', topic: 'ES', q: 'Scope creep in AgilePM is described as a change in the:', options: ['Depth of a requirement inside agreed scope', 'Breadth of requirements — adding, removing or significantly altering the overall list — which must be formally controlled', 'Colour of a burn-down chart', 'Length of the Daily Scrum'], correct: 'Breadth of requirements — adding, removing or significantly altering the overall list — which must be formally controlled', explain: 'After Foundations, the Solution Backlog baseline defines scope. Depth changes use MoSCoW contingency. Breadth changes threaten vision and control.' },
      { id: 'F30Q36', topic: 'ES', q: 'Consider the statements about house-building MoSCoW across increments.\n1. A requirement\'s priority at Project Increment level may differ from its priority for the whole project\n2. In a first increment, only foundations might be Must Have, with roof, walls and doors lower or Won\'t Have this time', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'MoSCoW applies at project, increment and Sprint levels. Importance is not the same as build order, but dependencies still matter for true Musts.' },
      { id: 'F30Q37', topic: 'ES', q: 'If a Project Organisation categorises everything as a Must Have, it often means:', options: ['MoSCoW is working perfectly', 'Requirements have not been broken down, or the people setting priorities lack experience, authority or time', 'Contingency has been maximised', 'Could Haves now exceed 20%'], correct: 'Requirements have not been broken down, or the people setting priorities lack experience, authority or time', explain: 'Even Must-Have epics usually contain lower-priority slices. Everything-is-Must destroys the buffer and raises failure risk.' },
      { id: 'F30Q38', topic: 'ES', q: 'Story points are used so that people of different skill levels can estimate together. Which psychological benefit does AgilePM also note?', options: ['They convert automatically into a Sponsor single-figure cost', 'Estimating effort rather than hours reduces fear of being blamed for inaccurate time predictions', 'They replace the Definition of Done', 'They allow the PM to impose the size'], correct: 'Estimating effort rather than hours reduces fear of being blamed for inaccurate time predictions', explain: 'Relative effort lets a professional and an amateur contribute without pretending they take the same elapsed time. Cards stay hidden to avoid anchoring.' },
      { id: 'F30Q39', topic: 'PC', q: 'Identify the missing agreement. The distinction between Should Have and Could Have can be subjective, so the Project Organisation should agree [ ? ] how those lesser priorities will be applied.', options: ['only after the final Deployment', 'at the start, ideally during Foundations', 'never, to keep debates lively', 'in a secret PM notebook'], correct: 'at the start, ideally during Foundations', explain: 'Agree criteria (people impacted, effort/benefit ratio) before heated delivery debates. Must Haves stay non-negotiable; the S/C line needs a shared rule.' },
      { id: 'F30Q40', topic: 'PC', q: 'Which of the following are true of Project Sprint Planning Topic 2 in AgilePM?\n1. Developers pull PBIs they forecast they can get Done toward the project Sprint Goal if one is used\n2. The Business Sponsor assigns every PBI to a named Developer\n3. PBIs are split between teams, typically with Scrum Masters facilitating\n4. MoSCoW is applied to selected PBIs and may change again in team Sprint Planning', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '1, 3, 4', explain: 'Self-management remains. The PM updates the Delivery Plan from the emergent understanding after teams share Sprint Backlogs.' },
      { id: 'F30Q41', topic: 'PC', q: 'In Delivery, building a thin end-to-end piece of user value — not a database now and a UI later — is described as:', options: ['A horizontal technology layer with no user value', 'A usable, end-to-end slice of value rather than a single technical layer', 'A Gantt row for documentation only', 'A Taylorist work breakdown by job title'], correct: 'A usable, end-to-end slice of value rather than a single technical layer', explain: 'Horizontal layers (database now, UI later) delay feedback and hide integration risk. Vertical slices support empiricism and incremental value.' },
      { id: 'F30Q42', topic: 'PC', q: 'Consider the statements about outcome versus output.\n1. Each Increment is an output because it is a deliverable\n2. The outcome is realised only when the product or solution is used in real-world operation', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Both 1 and 2 are true', explain: 'That is why Benefits Assessment sits on the value-realisation cycle after increments are deployed, and why charts of tasks are not enough.' },
      { id: 'F30Q43', topic: 'PC', q: 'If a complete Product Increment cannot be demonstrated at the end of a Sprint, AgilePM still expects teams to:', options: ['Hide the Sprint from stakeholders', 'Show progress in other honest ways (for example prototypes or test results) and improve discipline, integration or testing so the next Sprint yields something usable', 'Mark the work 100% complete for governance', 'Convert the DoD into optional guidance'], correct: 'Show progress in other honest ways (for example prototypes or test results) and improve discipline, integration or testing so the next Sprint yields something usable', explain: 'PAQ Statement 16: progress is primarily incremental demonstrable value. Even partial evidence beats a surprise at the end.' },
      { id: 'F30Q44', topic: 'RK', q: 'Identify the missing owner. The [ ? ] is responsible for identifying and owning Delivery Enablement risk, including project organisation, schedule and internal and external dependencies.', options: ['Business Visionary', 'Project Manager', 'Product Owner only', 'Business Advisor only'], correct: 'Project Manager', explain: 'BV owns Value Ownership risk; SA owns Solution Delivery risk; PM owns Delivery Enablement risk. They work the risks together.' },
      { id: 'F30Q45', topic: 'RK', q: 'Which of the following are true of PAQ Statement 17 commercial arrangements?\n1. Legal action after failure always restores the missed business opportunity\n2. Fixed-price for a fixed specification appears to transfer risk to the supplier but still burns time and opportunity if the project fails\n3. A fixed-price, fixed-scope contract will not function like a typical AgilePM project\n4. MoSCoW can be worked into more agile-friendly contracts', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '2, 3, 4', explain: 'Compensation does not return lost time or market window. If principles and roles would be seriously undermined, AgilePM may be the wrong approach.' },
      { id: 'F30Q46', topic: 'RK', q: 'If the PAQ shows not everyone understands the combined AgilePM and Scrum approach, and the team is not yet fully assigned (common in Feasibility), a typical remedy is:', options: ['Ignore the gap until the first Production outage', 'Schedule AgilePM training during Foundations', 'Replace MoSCoW with percentage-complete', 'Cancel the Product Owner role'], correct: 'Schedule AgilePM training during Foundations', explain: 'Statement 1 disagreement is usually a training or briefing gap. Use Feasibility to spot it and Foundations to fix it before major Delivery.' },
      { id: 'F30Q47', topic: 'GV', q: 'In the RACI model used in Appendix A, Responsible means the role that:', options: ['Must be informed after the fact only', 'Creates the artifact; Accountable means making sure the content is correct', 'May never read the artifact', 'Always equals the Business Sponsor for every artifact'], correct: 'Creates the artifact; Accountable means making sure the content is correct', explain: 'RACI = Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, Informed. One Accountable; Responsible does the creating. Do not swap R and A on ToR, SAD, DAD, MAD or backlogs.' },
      { id: 'F30Q48', topic: 'GV', q: 'Which snapshot and increment RACI assignments are correct?\n1. Feasibility Assessment — Responsible: Project Manager; Accountable: Business Sponsor\n2. Benefits Assessment — Responsible: Business Visionary; Accountable: Business Sponsor\n3. Product Increment — Responsible: Delivery Team; Accountable: Product Owner\n4. Sprint Backlog — Responsible: Business Sponsor; Accountable: Business Visionary', options: ['1, 2, 3', '1, 2, 4', '1, 3, 4', '2, 3, 4'], correct: '2, 3, 4', explain: 'Sprint Backlog is R=Developers A=Product Owner. Mixing Sponsor/Visionary onto the Sprint Backlog is a classic Foundation trap.' },
      { id: 'F30Q49', topic: 'GV', q: 'Identify the missing artifact. The [ ? ] is a Recommended evolutionary record, usually one document updated at the end of each Project Increment, confirming what was completed, what was not, lessons from the increment retrospective, and benefits that should now start to appear.', options: ['Sprint Backlog', 'Project Review Report', 'Daily Scrum', 'Planning Poker baseline story'], correct: 'Project Review Report', explain: 'R=Project Manager, A=Business Visionary. In regulated settings it is one of the artifacts that can be customised to show compliance. A full project retrospective is prepared after the final increment.' },
      { id: 'F30Q50', topic: 'GV', q: 'Consider the statements about responsible investment decisions.\n1. Responsible organisations still need to know projected cost and impact, expected return, and whether the next stage is worth pursuing\n2. Saying it feels like the right thing to do is treated as sufficient financial governance on its own in most organisations', options: ['Only 1 is true', 'Only 2 is true', 'Both 1 and 2 are true', 'Neither 1 nor 2 is true'], correct: 'Only 1 is true', explain: 'Financial governance can feel in tension with agility, but investment still needs evidence. Use ranges, MoSCoW, timeboxing, and keep the Business Case alive — stop if the Project Goal no longer makes sense.' },
    ],
  },
]
